      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>Foreword.</p>
        <p>Spending Christmas far from Warrington, and, for once in a while lacking the advantagg of a preliminary glance over the curreut inane of "The Examiner," I sit down to my weekly task feeling in the temporary absence of my usual source of inspiration that the reader may miss the comments suggested by contents of the paper. Hence this prefatory word of ex- planation. ,Now let -me proceed to do my hest in the situation. Christmas.</p>
        <p>I am writing on Christmas Day, and, with all xt,tx jealousy for the master's fair fame, I 04 myself 'somewhat inclined to question, the claim that Charles Dickens did more than any man to popularise the true ideal of this grestt feetival. True indeed it is that his sketches and stories, abound in family re-unions, chewy hearts, easy fireplaces, yonthful revelry, and above all the consideration for others which may well be said to be of the essence of. the Christmas spirit. But, as I have privieislY taken occasion to note, Washington Irving wrote .much of the pleasurable associations of this season—" the season of regenerated feel- ing—the season, ,for kindling, not merely the ire of hospitality in the hall, but the genial flame of charity in the heart" A phrase just quoted reminds me that Addison, too, in his delightful creation of Sir Roger de .Coverley, written in the earlier part of the. eighteenth ccitury, presents a charming Christmastide picture of a knight of the shire in his hall. "I' have often thought," writes he, "it hap- pens very well that Christmas ehould fall ant in the middle of the winter. It is the str.-.A atead and uncomfortable time of the year, when the poor people would 'suffer much from tl---cfr preverty and cold if they had not good cheer; 'arm fires, end Christmas gambols to suppart them. I love to rejoice their poor hearts at this Season, and to see the whole village merry in my great hall." And he goes on to show that he practically kept open house for twelve days at this period. So with all the old writers as far as my knowledge goes. I am aware . that Lord Macaulay used to tell of an elderly lady whom he knew while living with his parents at Clapham, who neither visited nor received visits frbm her neighbours, nor held any communication with them at Christmas. Moreover, her Christmas dinner invariably consisted of boiled mutton and apple-piei by way of protest against the general indulgence in roast beef and plum-pudding, which she utterly abhorred. Well, if a solitary individual cares thus to pose as the exception which proves the rule, one need hardly stop to waste pity. Besides, after all, it is only two cen- turies and a half since an austere Puritan .Asiembly tried to abolish the festival by its Act of Parliament, and though its member might as well have sought, with Canute, to arrest an advancing sea, they managed to exe..rt an effectually chilling influence upon the -season's material rejoicings.. It comes to this, then: these later writers re-discovered Christ- mas, and—yes, of course, Dickens played no unimportant part in the further revival wit, nessed in his day and generation. At the same time, truth compels the admission that the extravagant claim referred to in my opening sentence cannot be sustained, The simple lesson is that the happy may, so to express it, find ways and means to distribute .of their happiness without reducing their own total, merriment and goodwill being like the Widow's crnee of oil, which, often drawn upon, became no: less. Concerning Grace.</p>
        <p>Come, ye thankful people come, and let us consider some of the sayings of children en this subject. The topic is suggested by a caßital little story told by Mr. Ferens, M.P., set'ehairman at the Christmas festival of the Children's Home and Orphanage, founded by Dr. Bowman Stephenson. Pointing out how the increased cost of food told adversely in a great institution like that, he illustrated his point by a story of the little sun of a clergy. man who, when staying at a friend's houses wae asked to say grace—what father said be. fore beginning breakfast—and he surprised the friends at the table by repeating. "Go easy' with the bacCM! It's is. 6d. a pound—for that's what father always says," said the child. Then there is the old one of the two youngsters who eliSbuted as to whose father said the shorter "grace after meat" on Christmas Day. Said 'on.e, "Nobody could say a shorter one than my father, 'eci.he. just says 'Thank God."" Yes, my father's is shorter'n that," declared the ether. "He leans back in his chair, pulls down his waistcoat, and says *They-yer " A minister tells me of a little ehappie who' asked his mother why she didn't say grace like Mr. So4ind-So—a visitor on one occasion. "What did Mr. So-and-So say?" asked mamma. "Why he said, Bless this food to our use, and take us to the circus!'" Evidently "sanctify for Thy service," sounded something to do with the. "circus" then visiting the town, and of wliieh. the youngster's mind was full.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>When?</head>
        <p>When?</p>
        <p>Just as I completed the previous parsgraph the belated post-girl shot my paper into the letter-box, so that "The Examiner" has reached me after all. Well, well. What I have written I have written; let it Gtaud, I see that in Lymm notes "Critic" trwts that we may have achieved a triumphant victory before next Christmas. It reminds we th-at last April I met an old acquaintance who has prospered as a professional accountant, and he confided to me that the war would be over by the following June. I congratulated him on his sanguine disposition, and admitted that I was not myself so hopeful. "Ah," said my cocksure friend, "you may take in word for it:-I have what may be termed inside infor- mation, and there can be no doubt at all about it. I have a substantial bet on it, and it's a sure thing." He lives at a distance, and I have not ,seen him since, " Critic " does well to allow p:enty of margin.</p>
        <p>"Cause for Consolation." The timid man's expreasion of relief as re- corded,under this heading on page G of the raper before me as I write recalled that of an old lady in similar circumstances. Said the</p>
