        <p>9 ezErETzrimiesztrimmismangsm kr, 4 • For Children Cutting their Teeth. Prevent Convulsions. . . " ,4 .., • - Are Cooling and Soothing. - :*; •", 4- Contain nothing injurious to a tender limbo.</p>
        <p>I. ? Sold Everywhere in Stamped Boxes, Is. 3d. and 35., with Full Directions, ,- . Safe 4.,...k,,.,,_. xr.. -4t...„1g ''#A, t* a __VA t" - t$ 4. -A' -.... '1,1.7 7y, az,, Nk , ~,,k4 -z.,,,, Easy ..---1, ',-, - ~ bt 1,-6,1, Teething! I. 7- - ~.04:" , _ '''.. --1,,,,. V 4-!, Teething! ..,.. ~ ',. 2, - , ".. ,c, *„.z, (Alone have the Trade Mark: A RA "A BABY IN CDLE.") ...... . 0E a s</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <p>lecturer, "Nine million four hundred years from now, our coalfields will be entirely ex- hausted." "Dear, dear!" she exclaimed, showing genuine alarm," whatever shall we have to do then?" I myself had real "cause for sonsolation on a memorable night some 60 years ago when, reporting a lecture between six and seven miles from home, I found that the speaker had enraged a cab and that I might share with him; but the contrary when, attending at the same place in precisely simi- lar circumstances, and relying on a "lift" as before, I was informed at 10.20 p.m. that he had arranged to stop there for the night! Little Slips.</p>
        <p>Respecting "The new conductor," reproduced on page 3 of the last issue La. contemporary ob- served that to show him Tearing a glove (as</p>
        <p>I saw Sir Michael Costa do at a Leeds Musical Festival between 30 and 40 years ago) is an error, and went on: "Punch' is not always happy when dealing with orchestras. There was the case, some years ago, which attracted some attention, of the leader of the House who was represented as leading the second vio- lins, with the members of the then Govern- ment in serried rows behind him. But why stop with "punch" ? Such things might be multiplied almost iudefinitelY. The "Times" once idescribed the Crystal Palace organ (I think it was on the occasion of a Handel Festival) as possessing "wonderful ramifica- tions of fugues and diapasons." Black wrote of "Mozart's Sonata in A sharp"; and George Eliot, whom one seldom catches tripping, used the phrase "a long-drawn organ-stop." Superstition.</p>
        <p>As to that fortune-telling business, I know e. man who, although he would never dream of consulting a witch, constantly carries in hig pocket a potato as a charm against rheuma- tism. And what of those men (we have all seen them) who wear earrings as-a remedy for sore eyes? Some swear by cold tea as an eye lotion. But go to a professional fortune-teller! They would "scorn the haction." Reading.</p>
        <p>Just a few 'supplementary remarks on this subject. To become acquainted with the beet writers of all time is to become a citizen of the world indeed. "What is the use of a book without pictures And conversations?'? asks Alice in Lewis Carroll's immortal tale, but of course fierious readers must get far beyond that. ,I-would have one begin by reading novels exclusively if otherwise he or she would not read at all; for I believe many such would in that wayl come to "deepei sink and higher rise and to perfection grow." Still, a great deal of thetenormous mass of fiction nowadays published seems to be of the order described by Lowell as "literature suited to desolate islands." "Read the Bible reverently and attentively," said Sir Matthew Hale, "set your heart upon it, and lay it up in your memory, and make it the direction of your life: it will make you both a wise and a good man." Many besides Ruskin and John Bright have read it for general culture. Indeed, one can only be- come acquainted with the world's literature and art by reading the Bible much and often. The best of all art, whether in music, paint- ing, or architecture, has bees inspired by the Bible. Many are lest in art galleries and do not understand the meaning of the greatest pictures ever exhibited, because they do not know the contents of their Bible. Besides reading for general culture, be sure to read for special purposes. Every trade and profes- sion is associated with a history of its own, having its books of a technical character, and every man should mai ia his own line of busineFs to secure the best and latest informa- tion. I should like to enlarge here, but must postpone what little I wish to add.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>JAMIE, THE CALF.</head>
        <p>JAMIE, THE CALF.</p>
        <p>Striking Cireumstanees That Altered</p>
        <p>The Case.</p>
        <p>In Perthshire it is a custom for servants to call any cow or calf that their masters may chance to pnrehase, either by the name of the town from which it came, or by the name and surname of its pre-rictus owner. The following Is a good illustration of this practice. A fine specimen of the British fanner once lived not tar from the lively village of St. Martin's. This worthy old gentleman bought a calf from an elderly neighbour named Storer, but before he reached home with kis purchase the shades of evening had closed around him, and his family and domestic servants had retired to rest. Dis- daining to disturb either family or servants, he proceeded to the byre, and bound the calf with his own hands. On entering the house he in- formed his elder son of what he had done, and observed that he would see it in the morning. On the following morning the son went to the byre to see the calf, but was surprised to find that it was dead.. He immediately went to his father, and with a sorrowful face informed him that Jamie Storer was dead. "Oh, never mind," said the sire; "Jamie was an oddish sort of a man; you conldna look for onything else. In feet, I aye thocht he wad gang awa like the snuffing s' a candle, Never mind, never mind." "Ay, but fatber,". exclaimed the son, "it's Jamie Storer the calf that's dead!" "Jamie Storer the calf !" shouted the venerable • sire; "that alters the case. Bring me my slippers!" an order which was speedily obeyed. In company they reached the byre, only to find the sad tale verified. Poor Jamie bad been rather sleek hound, and in his struggles to free leimself he war eheked. Proof.</p>
        <p>It was the rush hour in one of those quick lunch places where you help yourself and use the arm of your chair as a table. A man called for a piece of pie and chose a chair; then, re- membering that he wanted coffee, he dashed aver to the service counter. When he returned with his" his Chair was-occupied by another hurry-up diner. " "Excuse me," said the first man, "but this is my chair." "How do you know it is your chair?" de- manded the oceupant, in a surly tone. . "Because I can prove it," stated the first man.</p>
        <p>"How can yen 'trove it?" asked the occu- pant. "You</p>
        <p>"By Your tre!y..”- ti)o“ reply. 11.1% sitting on my pie."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, DECEMBER 30, 1916.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, DECEMBER 30, 1916.</p>
        <p>MATTERS POLITICAL</p>
        <p>Guardians Decline To Meddle With Recruiting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>QUESTION OF PRINCIPLE</head>
        <p>A QUESTION OF PRINCIPLE</p>
        <p>Should the Board of Guardians confine itself simply to Poor Law Administration and refrain from discussing questions of national import- ance?</p>
        <p>At their meeting on Wednesday the Clerk (Mr. A. Bottomley) read a letter from the Hammer- smith Guardians asking for support of a resolu- tion protesting against the calling up of mar- ried men with families while single men of mili- tary age remain in factories. The Rev. A. M. Mitchell was soon support- ing the resolution, declaring that there were young men simply shirking in factories. It was also a matter of economy that they should join up before married men. Besides, it was very cruel to wives and children.</p>
        <p>The Chairman (Mr. A. J. Jolley) doubted whether the matter was in the Board's pro- vince. •</p>
        <p>Mr. W. Furness: If it is, I will second it</p>
        <p>The Chairman: There are some matters about which there is a wide divergence of opinion, and about which we as a Board are not immediately concerned.</p>
        <p>The Reverend Gentleman: This is not politi- cal; otherwise I would deprecate it. It eeems to me that every public body ought to be speak- ing out boldly on this matter:. If we don't do it now it will be too late.</p>
        <p>The Chairman: I don't say it is out of order, but I rather deprecate going beyond our pro- vince.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. C. Brooks: There are certain authori-</p>
        <p>ties wile are dealing with this question, and it would be rather an interference on our part to do anything like this. Two voted for the resolution and four against on the principle laid down by the Chairman.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>IS IT CONVENIENT ?</head>
        <p>IS IT CONVENIENT ?</p>
        <p>Rev. A. M. Mitchell Wants The</p>
        <p>Guardians' Meetings Altered.</p>
        <p>Taking an active interest ip the work of the Board of Guardians, the 'Rev. A. M. Mitchell put forward another motion at the meeting on Wednesday morning. Dissatisfied with the attendances at the fort- nightly meetings, which are held on every other Wednesday morning, he moved that they be held at three o'clock on alternate Friday afternoons. He said he always regretted that their meetings were not held in ther evenings like the Town Council, but he supposed that as many members of the Board resided out of town that was im- practicable. The present time, however, cer- thinly required alteration, 10.30 on Wednesday mornings being a little awkward for most, very inconvenient for some, and impossible for a few, among whom was Mr. Dale. It was in the in- terests of those that 'he (the reverend gentle- man) acted. • There was only just a quorum—seven—present on this occasion, and no seconder being forth- coming, the motion collapsed. "it is very unfortunate," remarked Mr. Mitchell, as his resolution was metaphorically thrown into the waste paper basket, "because, you see, those who would second it cannot get here."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>LEATHER.</head>
        <p>LEATHER.</p>
        <p>WAR OFFICE TAKES OVER ALL SUPPLIES</p>
        <p>The Army Council give notice that it is their intention to take possession of all leather of the classes mentioned in the following schedule which is now in stock in the United Kingdom whether in the finished state or in course of production, and of all leather of the same classes which can be produced between the date hereof and Itlrach 31, 1917. If after this notice any person having control of any such leather, without the consent of the Army Council, sells, moves, or secretes such leather, or deals with it in any way contrary to any condition imposed in any licence, permit, or order that may be granted in respect thereof, he shall be guilty of an offence against the De- fence of the Realm (Consolidation) Regulations, 1914.</p>
        <p>Applications for permission to sell or remove such leather should be addressed to the Director of Army Contracts, Imperial House, Tuthill- street, Westminster, S.W. The schedule comprises the following leather produced from all classes of hides, British or imported :- 1. Vegetable-tanned bends of 10Th. and up- wards; vegetable-tanned butts of 201 b. and up- wards; vegetable-tanned backs of 141 b. and up- wards.</p>
        <p>2. Chrome-tanned bonds. butts and backs having a substance of 7-iron and upwards. 3. Vegetable-tanned shoulders of 6-iron and upwards on the cut edge.</p>
        <p>4. Kip butts of 11 m.m. substance and up- wards.</p>
        <p>5. Kip sides of any finish except semi-chrome. 6. , Vegetable-grained upper leathers of 11 ni.m. substance and upwards. , 7. Chrome upper leather of 11 m.m substance and upwards,</p>
        <p>8. . Upper leathers of Im.m. substance and upwards; —raanufactured by any other process which has been approved by the Chief Inspect- ing Officer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>TANNING TRADE TRIBUTE.</head>
        <p>TANNING TRADE TRIBUTE.</p>
        <p>The splendid tribute paid to the tanning trade by Mr. Wise, of the War Office Department, at the meeting of the Tanners' Federation, held on December Bth, is highly complimentary, and speaks well for the way in which tanners have met the wishes and demands of the Contracts Department in every way possible.- . There is a very busy time. ahead, and signs of this are apparent judging by the number of inquiries that have been received for the supply of leather as far torward as March, but tanners are not at liberty to accept any contracts for delivery after December 31st, unless same have been approved of by the R.A.C.D., as the order for Russian and ether boots has not yet been placed with the manufacturers. From all accounts,' tanners are more or less well in hand with present contracts, and with the exception of the heavier weights in bends and shoulders they seem likely to be in a good position to meet the dethands when they come along. 'Regarding the civilian trade, and prices for January-February delivery, the general ten- dency is higher, though in the main any. Vance will not, be. a Very appreciable one.—War- rington Correspondent of the "Leather Trades Review."-</p>
        <p>BABY'S WHEEZING. Asthmatic Bronchitis Cured By Veno's Lightning. Cough Cure.</p>
