      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>4.• + .4.. •:* .:. • .:•.:-, .2. •:. ‘.4:7 ~:,,,',.....:-..:•.:, -,:. +•,:.•:..“).:.•:•.).:..C...).:. ..:••:,...:..:&gt;.7.,..:...).:•-:.•:',.-::“:.•:•..".•:. + + 4.:. &lt;::. • 11'..:. • •:••:-.:, (.:4 -:- .:4 ..:•.:..) ••:.:•.:••:•••4:••:••::....).:••:... ••:.••:•••:••:...:••:••:••:•.:...T...44:.&lt;••:••:••:- ..:...: t..... • ..) 4. ..:.. /Ai 0 ..., 1 • 4.. 40 4. ... to. : ' '. PiN'S WORLD. .:...:...... ...:..:. • . •</head>
        <p>4.• + .4.. •:* .:. • .:•.:-, .2. •:. ‘.4:7 ~:,,,',.....:-..:•.:, -,:. +•,:.•:..“).:.•:•.).:..C...).:. ..:••:,...:..:&gt;.7.,..:...).:•-:.•:',.-::“:.•:•..".•:. + + 4.:. &lt;::. • 11'..:. • •:••:-.:, (.:4 -:- .:4 ..:•.:..) ••:.:•.:••:•••4:••:••::....).:••:... ••:.••:•••:••:...:••:••:••:•.:...T...44:.&lt;••:••:••:- ..:...: t..... • ..) 4. ..:.. /Ai 0 ..., 1 • 4.. 40 4. ... to. : ' '. PiN'S WORLD. .:...:...... ...:..:. • . •</p>
        <p>from thoee so wonderfully produced by the ?workers. The Mayfair organisation has npw been at work for several 'years, and the industry is in a flourishing condition. The show was ex- ceptionally good this year, and included not only imitation cut flowers, but flourishing plants in handsome gilt baskets and beautiful specimens of fruit, all of which were as faith- fully imitated as the flowers, and most attrac- tively displayed. For some time past it has been the fashion to arrange roses, lotus lilies, and water lilies in shallow bowls—black for preference—the blossoms floating on the water. At the Mayfair Show the most charming of the exhibits were these flowers, arranged in glass bowls, and rendered in the making impervious to water. The Christmas roses, pots of mistle- toe, crocuses and snowdrops, were really beauti- ful works of art.</p>
        <p>Return of the Military Hat. The military note which was so painfully overdone when. war broke out, has returned with the latest millinery, and may be noted in several new hats, one of which is almost an exact imitation of the Hussar cap, with the black brash in front as its sole adornment. Another hat that comes from Paris is evidently inspired by the Russian Cossack busby, and a third owes its origin to the British busby. All three have high crowns. The Serbian cap is also shown, trimmed with very brilliant -em- broideries. Some very curious hats are of Eastern origin, and resemble a saucer, with a slight rise instead of a depression in the centre, and has turned up brims. -Worn slightly tilted forward it is not becoming to every woman. Hats of Eastern types are made of thick materials, such as satin, brocade, and suede leather, and are trimmed with rich embroid- eries more especially the peacock eye. Other hats have swathings of cloth, or white suede, round a turban shape, but the latter are of the toque variety,* and are European in style. A very smart hat, when properly putt on, is the shape worn -by Francis I. and his courtiers, which, however, is minus the long feather affected by the King and gentlemen of France.</p>
        <p>Eastern Influences on Fashionable Modes.</p>
        <p>Fashion were probably never more influenced by Eastern modes than at the present moment. The cashmere shawl is having quite a .vogue in sympathy possibli with the Paisley, which has lately been revived enthusiastically. It is quite fifty years since the lovely Cashmere shawlstl were worn, but there must be a large number still in existence', and in a good state of pre- i servation, for they were prized as a valuable wedding present of Queen Victoria to her maids of honour and friends. Many of the new even- ing ,dresses bear a strong resemblance to the robes' of ancient Egyptian women, and the paintings on mummy cases have evidently pro- vided ideas for these modern gowns. A new sleeve which is English in origin might easily be mistaken for a long evening glove. It reaches above the elbow, is scalloped at the edges, and in order that it may stand out from the arm is stiffened with fine wire. For a simple frock it gives a certain distinction, al- though it is more curious than elegant. There is usually a puffed sleeve above this long one, 'of ninon Georgette, or some other transpar- ency.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>CHAT and GOSSIP.</head>
        <p>CHAT and GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>By MARJORIE.</p>
        <p>A Friend in Need.</p>
        <p>Quite an army of women of all classes banded together two years ago and. undertook to do many little things that come in the train of a big war. The work is valuable and wide in scope, but it is so quietly done that only a few people are aware of the existence of the organisation. The working wives of the men at the Front know little of the business side of the war, and it is to give them the help they need with clerical and other matters that ladies ;go daily from the West to the East End of London, where clearing houses have been estab-</p>
        <p>lished for these matters. There are certain complicated regulations in connection with drawing allowances and pensions which puzzle and annoy the average wife and mother of the private soldier, for she does not understand how to fill in the Government slips, nor where to send them. Assistance is given in these and other matters. The whole lives of these women have been changed, and at the beginning it was difficult for them to adapt themselves to the new conditions, hence the need for these</p>
        <p>helpers. who also charge themselves with pro- viding food and ,clothing when necessary. Sym- pathy and labour are freely given, and the two years of work unostentatiously carried out, has brought comfort to hundreds of war widows and relatives of soldiers.</p>
        <p>The Light Clothing Industry. ,Before the war thousands of men were em- ployed in the various departments of the light clothing industry, which includes the making of' corsets, shirts, collars and every kind of underclothing. Substitution of women for men in the making of corsets has been tried for some months now, and a pamphlet dealing with the work of the women with various machines</p>
        <p>has been recently published. Much of the cut- ting out is done by a machine, which resembles a guillotine, whilst the pressing is done by elec- trical and steam power. The women have proved successful with ,each of these machines, although the work is laborious. They have also done the work of the printing room, used the shears and various large knives, kept the store- rooms and warehouses, and loaded up the goods. For some of this work a short training has been necessary. In many cases women who have been employed on the sewing machines have been taught to work the guillotine, their places being filled by less experienced hands. In the underclothing deparpnent the substi- tutes have been equally successful, and women are now using the various knives and shears as well as doing the work of the warehouses and storerooms.</p>
        <p>Wonderful Certified Flowers and Fruit.</p>
        <p>The girls employed in the Mayfair flower- making industry, unlike those who work for millinery, copy exactly the natural blooms. Each girl works from a specimen, perfect of its kind. So cleverly are the \blossoms imitated that it is difficult to distinguish the real flowers</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>BOOKS CHILDREN LIKE TO READ.</head>
        <p>BOOKS CHILDREN LIKE TO READ.</p>
        <p>ILY DEAR CHILDREN, By asking you to tell me which books you would like if you gained a prize I have found out, tcv a large extent, the kind of books you like to read. I am sure the selection you have made is an admirable one, and I will bear it in mind when I am buying your books in future. I hope you will all read the letters I have received, as they are very instructive and thoughtful. The prize goes to</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>1028 Niece Bessie Randles,</head>
        <p>1028 Niece Bessie Randles,</p>
        <p>76, Cartwright-street, who attends Evelyn- street School. This week we will have a letter on</p>
        <p>How I Spent Christmas Day.</p>
        <p>That is a sUbject you will all be able to write about. I intend to give a prize for those up to ten years of age and one for those over ten years of age. You must reckon yourselves ten until your eleventh birthday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>SOME ADVICE TO COMPETITORS.</head>
        <p>SOME ADVICE TO COMPETITORS.</p>
        <p>Niece Nancy Adams says she often wonders what time letters to the Corner have to be delivered. I like to get them on lifondtiy, Tuesday and Wednesday, so that I can have plenty of time to read them and decide which are the best. Thursday Is altogether too late,</p>
        <p>as the Editor has to make up his paper then and get ready for Saturday. So I shall be glad to get letters as early as possible and not later than Wednesday morning. I should also like you all to state clearly and fully your number, name and address, and school. Ido my best, but I cannot re- member all these facts about more than a thousand nephews and nieces, can I? Your loving UNCLE JACK.</p>
        <p>THE WINNING LETTER. By Niece Bessie Bandies.</p>
        <p>"76, Cartwright-street, Warrington, Decem- ber 26th, 1916. "Dear Uncle Jack,—l have never been lucky enough to win a prize, but if I should succeed in doing so I would very much like a book called 'Sunday,' because if we read that book we will learn a great many lessons. Another reason why I like it is because it teaches us to be good and kind to all animals.—l remain, your loving niece, 1028 BESSIE RANDLES (age 11), Evelyn-street School."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>HIGHLY COMMENDED.</head>
        <p>HIGHLY COMMENDED.</p>
        <p>Nephew Torn Siddle, 70, Battersby-lane.—"l would very much like a school tale, something similar to 'His First Term,' by Finnemore; or 'Tom Brown's School Days.' This interests schoolboys better than anything in my opinion. Historical events are very suit- able for boys when they are ;Ts k e d anything to do with history; they will know something about it. Another reason for a book like this is that they live in England and should know the origin of 'England and its founders." Niece Gladys Morris, 60, Priestley-street (Evelyn-street School).—" The book I like best is A Basketwof Flowers,' because it is suitable</p>
        <p>for a girl and has a good meaning. For in- stance, it just Shows the jealousy of people and teaches us never to be selfish."</p>
        <p>Niece Lily McLachlin, 8, Ash Grove, Latch- ford (Bolton Council School).--" A particular book I would like very much is Lamb's 'Tales from Shakespeare.' A number of the tales tell about famous men in back history. Other tales are about Kings and Jews. On, Jew was called Shylock. He was to cut a pound of flesh from Antonio's body. .Shylock earned his thing by lending money and charging inter- est and getting' the same amount of money he lent back. He was very hard and inean, keen to his' business. This is only one of the tales I have read, and I hope I am successful in winniiilt the book I have wrote about."</p>
        <p>Nephew John Ditchfield, 55, Orford-lane (Hamilton-street School), says he would like a history of the Great War.</p>
        <p>Niece Lizzie Lewis, 21, Samuel-street (Evelyn-street School).—"My favourite book is tht Bible. It teaches us to be good and teaches us many a lesson, which we ought to remember. The Bible is a very useful book, and some people could not get on in the world without it. I like the part about Elijah„and Ahab, the wicked king, and his wife Jezebel. Another nice part is about Jesus Christ and the three wise men who followed the star, and, by following it, reached the stable in which Jesus was born."</p>
        <p>Niece Nancy Adams, 27, Samuel-street.—" I think I will choose 'Her Benny.' I was told that it is about little Arabs of the streets in their homes. They are .not all bad, as many people seem to think. Many of them try hard to earn an honest living, though they find it a difficult matter, especially when at home; they receive no encouragement, while in the streets temptation is being continually put in their way by those of whom 'Perks' so justly complains in the story. The groupings of the characters that figure in the story are purely fictitious, but not the characters themselves. 'Benny,' Little Nell,' 'Perks,' Jr Wag,' 'Granny,' and 'Eva Lawrence' are drawn from life."</p>
        <p>Nephew Harry Heywood, 8, Matthews-street (Parochial School), who kindly sends his best wishes to me and Uncle Ned, as so many other nephews and nieces have done, says that he won a prize with an essay on 'Lord Kitch- ener. He now writes: "I would like 'With Kitchener at the Soudan,' because Lord Kitch- ener was a true Englishman and he died for England. The book contains many tales of heroism."</p>
        <p>Nephew William Northall, 69, Hardy-street (Hamilton-street School).—" 'Happy Days.' I like this book because there is 'a nice tale in it about a boy and a girl. They were in the nursery one fine summer day and at the time were quarrelling. The day before their mother had bought the boy a story-book and the girl a Teddy hew-. The boy, interested in a tale, was disturbed by his sister, and the boy with anger tore her Teddy in pieces and threw it through the nursery window. A week after the girl went to a toy shop and bought her brother a story-book, and at this same time the boy bought his sister a Teddy bear, and in the evening met in the garden and were friends once again." Nephew H. Lloyd, 110, Hardy-street.—" The book I would prefer is 'Kenilworth,' by Sir Walter Scott, whose stories I like very much."</p>
        <p>Niece Clara Connor, 8, Mount-street.—"lf I should be a lucky one to win a prize I would like the book 'Chatterbox.' My cousin ,has one. When I go to her house I get so inter- ested that I would like one of my own, be- cause the tales are funny, interesting and instructing. The pictures are so beautifully coloured that it makes me want one all the more."</p>
        <p>Nephew Joe Riley, 11, Ford-street.—" I wOuld</p>
        <p>like a history book, if it is not too expenBi7e, because I like reading about history at school." Nephew H. James, 121, Longford-street, writes to say his soldier-brother has been home</p>
        <p>for Christmas and his "Dad " is ill at Great Yarmouth. Nephew H. James has three bro- thers. in the Army, so his home is doing great things for the country. We all hope the war will soon be over and that happier times will then come for all of us.</p>
        <p>Nephew Ernest Taylor, 63, Hardy-street (Sil- ver-street School).—" If it came my luck to win a prize I should prefer the good old book, Uncle Tom's Cabin.' I was on ray holidays at Blackpool last slimmer and I heard a great deal of talk about 'Uncle Tom's Cabin.' I *mild like to pea il the book oil how the slaves were treated and how slavery came to be abolished."</p>
        <p>THANKS FOR PRIZES.</p>
        <p>Niece Gladye Morris, Evelyn-street School, writes:—</p>
        <p>"I must thank you for the beautiful hook you sent me. I cannot tell you my thanks well enough; but if I try harder I may have the luck to win one or two more prizes." Niece Lily McLachlan, 8, Ash-grove, writes:</p>
        <p>"Dear Uncle Jack,—T received your kind present on Saturday at noon, which I was highly pleased with. Mother was surprised to see I had won again. I have read nearly all the tales, and I have enjoyed reading them.— I remain, your loving niece, LILY MCLACHLAN." "70, Battersby-lane, Warrington, December 25th, 1916.</p>
        <p>"Dear Uncle Jack,—Many thanks for the</p>
        <p>prize you awarded to me last week. It is very interesting, as far as I have read, and most suitable.—From 1033 Nephew Tom SIDDLE."</p>
        <p>NEW MEMBERS.</p>
        <p>1120 Niece Lizzie Hales, 66, Priestley-etreet; age 10; Priory School.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>. / tA, V il' 2 ' , ' ~, 4 % ). 4400</head>
        <p>. / tA, V il' 2 ' , ' ~, 4 % ). 4400</p>
        <p>ONLY TO TH</p>
        <p>IN K.. TH</p>
        <p>LH E_LM</p>
        <p>Soft strains of memory's music stir my soul As children's songs come floatihg back to me, Flooding anew my life with sunshine warm Sweet as the breath of Eden—and as free.</p>
        <p>Two noble, bright-eyed boys of four and seven, Brimful of fun and frolic all the day; With thoughtful questionings oft they came to me- They could not understand our grown-up way.</p>
        <p>I see again the playthings thickly strewn On nursery floor, or parlour, steps and hall; From blocks a Palace high and grand has sprungl, Whose many spires divide the towering wall.</p>
        <p>I hear them calling in their happy play To mamma, "Come and see our new boat sail; See how she proudly holds her pretty mast, Now watch her ride erect the stifrning gale."</p>
        <p>Soft llght and shadow chase about my chair, AS dreamily I watch them come and go, But block and toy with palace wall have flown; E'en eail has gone, whose port I never knew. I -</p>
        <p>.nsteadtof little boys with qUestionings strange, Who could not understand the.thinge of life— Two earnest men with thoughtful, loving gaze Lock in-my eyes and scan my face to-night.</p>
        <p>She Felt the Same. Ile' gazed longingly into her eyes. "I am very, very fond of you, Miss Evelyn," lie whispered. "Then we, shall get along wonderfully: I am very fond of myself."</p>
        <p>(Regd. Trade Alan!.)</p>
        <p>FM ESSE</p>
