      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <p>OUTWITTING THE PRINCE REGENT.</p>
        <p>When Sheridan was bard up for cash he used to make his wit earn the means for subaistence, and he could accomplish this in no better way than by laying wagers with the Prince Regent. A discussion once arose as to the respective walking capabilities of turkeys and geese, and the wit expressed himself very incredulous of the assertion that turkeys would walk twice as far as geese in a given space of time. Re effected to believe that geese could be driven as fast on the road as turkeys, and the thing ap- peared so very absurd, that the Prince instantly laid him a heavy wager that suoh was not the case. Sheridan, however, knowing the " early- to-bed " habits of turkeys, took care to arrange a long distance to be traversed, and to fix the period of trial late in the afternoon. The eonse- quence was that evening set in before the rival flocks had anything like arrived at their destination, and although the turkeys out- stripped the geese at first, as the shades of night fell they refused to be driven forward, and got flying up on the branches of the trees at the road side. The geese, 'however, were drives steadily forward to the winning point, and the Prince Regent lost his wager.</p>
        <p>Mamma Didn't Say.</p>
        <p>"Mamma, what is the new baby named?" "It has no name, my dear." "Well, how do we know it is ours, then?"</p>
        <p>One for the Censor.</p>
        <p>An extract from a sailor's letter to his wife: "Dear Jane,—l am sending you a postal order for 10s., which I hope you may get—but you may not—as this letter has to pass the censor."</p>
      </div>
