      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>CONTROL OF THE DRINK</head>
        <p>CONTROL OF THE DRINK</p>
        <p>TRADE.</p>
        <p>Early, swift and drastic action in the direc- tion of further restricting the sale of liquor is 'promised from many quarters. " Those who are interested in it," says "The Times," re- ferring ominously to a strong and growing demand for this action, " will do well to recognise the signs and be prepared to accept tno inevitable." What is the inevitable? Is it to be additional restrictions under the very exhaustive powers we already possess? Is it to be State purchase and State control? Or is it to be prohibition? The method of treat- -merit is the one that remains for settlement; that some treatment is necessary and is about 'to be applied may be accepted as one of the certainties of the igituation. No interests ought to be allowed to stand in the way of the national wellbeing; and the liquor traffic stands glaringly in the way of the country now at war for sheer existence, in three essetntial particulars. In the first place it makes shock- ing use of food material when food is scarce and the supply, in some directions, approaches famine conditions. In the second place, it needlessly absorbs man power, In the third, place it interferes sadly in many places with the duo dispatch of work of national import- ance. These are points that are now accepted all round as a true statement of the case. We have travelled a long and hard road before acknowledging so much, but a country that has accepted conscription as inevitable may, well be in a position to see how drink, in the memorable words of Mr. LLOYD GEORGE, may at the present juncture be a greater enemy than either Germany or Austria.</p>
        <p>There exist several very effective ways of dealing with the problem. The Food Control- ler Might further limit the amount of raw material used in the manufacture of liquor; the Sugar ...omrnisaion might further regu- late the supply of sugar for the purposes of the Trade; the Board of Control might fur- ther restrict the hours of sale and close houses in certain areas; the Shipping' Controller might prohibit the carriage of materials for brewing and distilling; and the Director of National Service might schedule the Trade as a dispensable occupation. Nothing more drastic than the application of one of the above powers, or a combination of them, could be conceived; in faCt, they raise visions of popsibly extreme measures that might, even now, raise 'a storm of resentment that would do as much harm as the restrictions would do good. To find out exactly what to do is Mr. LLOYD Onown's great opportunity. He thought he had that opportunity nearly twcb years ago, but for some reason that opportu- nity passed by. In the meantime we have had the experience of the Control Board in Munition areas where 'drunkenness has de- creased and work upon munitions has not suffered to anything like the former extent. 1 Prohibition, which is not advocated only by; extreme temperance workers, but by other men of position and influence, does not find general favour. The State purchase of the drink trade interests and the public control of its business is, on the other hand, urged by an increasing vsinjne of public opinion. Althofigh there are grave objections to this line of action nationalisation would give ars al chance of dealing with a went public menace ' as a whole on on aqaitable basis. It would also dear the way, if ever ikt time abook.l 10 ripe, for hiLi</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
