      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <p>BIRTHS, MARRIAGES, DEATHS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>"THE EXAMINER" HD.</head>
        <p>"THE EXAMINER" HD.</p>
        <p>Increased Cost Of Production Causes Increase In Price.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>GOOD VALUE FOR MONEY.</head>
        <p>GOOD VALUE FOR MONEY.</p>
        <p>Since the outbreak of the war the cost of printing paper and other material used in the production of" The Examiner" has increased at a serious rate.</p>
        <p>We have hitherto resisted the temptation to raise the selling price to our customers in the hope that there would soon be an improve- ment in the markets. In the meantime we have maintained the standing of "The Examiner" as a newspaper and have even gone to additional expense by giving a liberal supply of illustrations, /especially photo- graphs, which we re-produce in a manner acknowledged to be unsurpassed in British journalism. Contrary to expectation, costs continue to ascend, and this week we have received an in- timation from our paper-makers that</p>
        <p>In consequence of the further restrictions on raw material and therefore increased cost of production we are obliged tc advance the price of paper . . . . to a figure which makes" it nearly three times the cost of the same paper in July,-1914. In view of these facts we are compelled increase the price of "The Examiner" to lid. a copy. • "The Examiner " .is well worth lid., and we shall endeavour to keep it at a high level of excellence.</p>
        <p>The change will take effect on and from next Saturday, January 6th, 1917.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>GOSSIP.</head>
        <p>GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>The result of the Christmas haul of excessive drinkers by the local police force, reinforced for the occasion by a number of zealous special con- stables, was, happily, almost insignificant.</p>
        <p>Saturday was practically Christmas Eve, on Sunday the houses were open as usual, and on Monday (Christmas Day) the customary facili- ties for imbibing were forthcoming; but on Tues- day morning (Boxing Day) the Police Court was a dreary prospect, and only three or four per- sons (addresses unknown) answered the charge for over-indulgence.</p>
        <p>It is interesting to note that although exact -figures are not forthcoming just new as to con- victions for drunkenness during the year, it is safe to assume that there has been at the fairest computation at least a twenty-five per Cent. lower list than in 1915. The figures In 1915 were 586, being 29 less than in 1914. 'Chief Constable Nicholls agrees that the.-Christmas.just over has been the Quietest as regards street 'disturbances that he has known.</p>
        <p>" It will be seen by "The Examiner" lighting- up table that the evenings are rapidly lengthen- ing. Between to-day and next Saturday light- ing-up times and obscuring times are extended by no less than seven minutes. " The mornings grow lighter more slowly, the si4i rising at the same time on both Saturdays, there having been only one minute variation in the mean- time. The turn has already been taken, how- ever, for lighter mornings as well as longer evenings.</p>
        <p>The attempt to make the streets more en- durable at night by giving a few additional rays of light, has been only partially successful.</p>
        <p>Why, for inetance; should••-the'vdtts, which could be obscured instantly in eat7e of need, be, allowed to creep about in.the' dark threatening all and sundry with deetruction?-1, • .</p>
        <p>Why should•not the electric lights, which can be instantly put• out, be allowed to remain in until eleven or twelve—in 'the absence of an alarm, of course. .</p>
        <p>Why—but why go on? It is sufficient to say there is much discontent at the why the. taln is not lighted. We are all willing to plough our way through darkness and lamp posts if it is necessary; but is it necessary? That, as Ham- let would say; is the question.</p>
        <p>The Board of Gnardians' meeting arranged for Wednesday morning only just tOok place. It nearly collapsed through want of numerical nourishment.</p>
        <p>A fortnight previously Mr. George Mather wanted it abandoned on the grou-nd that it was too near the holidays, and farmers usually car- ried on the Christmas celebrations throughout the week. Mr. W.- J. Shaw supported him; but the meeting was called .41,a agreed upon.</p>
        <p>So on Wednesday the Chairman (Mr. A. J. Jolley) was in his place at the appointed time, and had to wait threequarters of an hour before the attendance devekped into a quorum and business could proceed.</p>
        <p>Mr. ZoHey sat talking with the Clerk (Mr. Arthur Bottomley); the Rev. A. M. Mitchell playfully conversed with the Pressmen;, Mr. Siddelly read his "Sporting Chronicle," Mr. Flanagan looking over occasionally; while Mr. Furness, Mr. Brooks, and officials besieged the fire and made racy observations upon the foggy weather and that other foggy subject—the peace proposals.</p>
        <p>All this because they were one short of a quorum. The best man to get, it was suggested, was Mr. Shaw, who has a telephone at his elbow in his home, not a great distance away. So he was rung up, and, though he protested that he had a bad eold—avery bad cold—was persuaded</p>
        <p>to wrap himself up and come along. His arrival was heralded with a oheer.i Like the old gentle- man who upset the pepper pot, he coughed and sneezed in reply.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>And business proceeded apace.,</head>
        <p>And business proceeded apace.,</p>
        <p>Private 'J. Taylor, of Kendrick-street. the young D.C.M., of whom Warrington is so proud, called to see me one day this week. He is " fit " once more, and is soon due back with his battalion in training in this country. I understand his formd workmates at Smethurst's foundry entertained him last evening. '</p>
        <p>Death has been particularly busy this Christ- mas time, the varying and hard weather hav- ing found the weak spot in many constitutions.</p>
        <p>For the week ended December 23 there were 35 deaths in town against only 12 in the corre- sponding week Last year, and the high rate has, apparently, been kept up since.</p>
        <p>At the Cemetery there has been a most un- usual state of things. The numerous deaths and the shortage of labour owing to the giavediggers and gardeners having been °Lamed for fighting purposes, have made it impossible to cope ex- peditiously with interments, many of which have had to be postponed beyond the dates desired by the relatives.</p>
        <p>During the week I have mentioned three deaths were from influenza, four from pneu- monia, and four from bronchitis, consumption and measles accounting for a fair number.</p>
        <p>It ie, an the ahoy hand., satisfaetory to know that the foyer Nrepital has few patients new. So many ag 94 cki,i. of mrael,es were notified tagt week,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>al ITUR .1, . Y.</head>
        <p>al ITUR .1, . Y.</p>
        <p>MR. ALFRED GIBSON.</p>
        <p>Death Of Long-Service R3lands's Engineer.</p>
        <p>With forty-two years' • service to his credit as an engineer with the firm of Messrs. Rylands Bros., Ltd., Mr. Alfred Gibson passed away at his residence, 65, Manchester-road, on Christ- mas Day. Mr. Gibson had. a fall in his bed- room a fortnight last Thursday, but was able to venture out of doors for a few days. He took to his bed on the Wednesday before Christmas Day, and died, as stated, from in- fluenza and bronchial pneumonia. Mr. Gibson, who was 72 years of age, retired from the service of Messrs. Rylands Bros., Ltd., about eight years ago, having gained the high esteem of both employers and workmen. He leaves two sons, a daughter, and a grand- daughter. The funeral took place at the Warrington Cemetery on Thursday, the Rev. S. G. Stanton officiating. The body was borne to the grave by several of Mr. Gibson's old colleagues. The chief mourners were: Messrs. Joseph and truest Gibson (sons), Miss Lily Gibson (granddaughter), Messrs. James Atherton Gib- son (brother), James Gibson, jun., Albert Gib- son, .R. G. Turner and Jos. Garner (nephews), George Mason, B. Farrar, J. Webb, T. Rey- nolds and several representatives from Messrs. Rylands Bros. Beautiful floral tributes were placed on the grave from "All at home," " Joo and. Polly,"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>LATE MR. ALFRED 'GIBSON.</head>
        <p>THE LATE MR. ALFRED 'GIBSON.</p>
        <p>"Brother, nephew and niece," "Bob, Lena and Mary," Mi. J. W. Jeffery, Mr. and Mrs. T. P. Throssell, Mr. and Mrs. W. Whittaker, Mrs. Stretton and family, Mr. W. Mounfield and "Maggie," Mr. and Mrs. George Mason, "Harold and Kate" (Burnley), "Lena; Jessie and Dorothy," Mr. and Mrs. John Bell, Mr. and Mrs. Ashton, "Jim and Annie," Mr. and if rs. H. Wakefield, Councillor and Mrs. Archer, Mrs. Hartley and Miss Doherty, Mr. and Mrs.. J. Cobb, "From the Fitting Shops. Church- 'street and Dalton-bank," Employes of George Allen and Sons.</p>
        <p>_ • The funeral arrangements were in the hands of Messrs. J. Mounfield and sons.</p>
