      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>MR. JACOB SANKEY.</head>
        <p>MR. JACOB SANKEY.</p>
        <p>Ex-Councillor And Well-known Tradesman's Death.</p>
        <p>It is with regret that we announce the death of Mr. Jacob Sankey, aged 66 years, who passed away at his residence in Church-street, on Christmas morning.</p>
        <p>Mr. Sankey had been in failing health for rather a considerable time, but had only been confined to his room for a short period. Reared at Risky Old 'Hall, he came to War- rington When fifteen years old, to take part in a milk business, which was under the direction of Mrs. Prophet, and subsequently took full control. Well-established, the business has been a flourishing one for many years, Mr. Sankey haring displayed considerable business acumen in its maintenance and development- Of a reticent nature, it was a considerable surprise to some when he allowed himself to be 'put forward for election in the Town Council, but, though he lost the • first contest, he later won the honours aud represented Howley Ward for about nine years, retiring in 1910. His work oh the'committees was greatly valued, especially his services as Chairman of the Sanitary Works Committee.</p>
        <p>In politics he was a conservative, and as AL Churchman was esteemed for his devotion, honesty of purpose and practical sympathy with any •phase of religious 'enterprise. The funeral took place at the Warrington Cemetery yesterday morning. The first part of the service was held in the Parish Church, the Rector and the Rev. T. Vickers officiating. Messrs. J. Mounfield and Sons carried out the arrangements.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>MR. W.</head>
        <p>MR. W.</p>
        <p>FA I ItHURST.</p>
        <p>Funeral Of \cll-knol% 11 Coin Merchant.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Cemetery, on Saturday morning, the funeral took place of Mr. William Fairhurst, of Rose Bank, Whitley, Cheshire, who died at Southport on the previous Thurs- day, after a few day? illness. The Rector (the Rev. Canon Willis) read the first part of the burial service at the Parish Church and was assisted by the Rev. J. C. Trampkasure (vicar of Whitley), who officiated at the graveside. The mourners were: Mr. Frank Fairhurst (son), Miss Fairhurst (daughter), Mr. •T. Fair- hurst, of Altrincham (nephew);, Mr. John Fairhurst, of Warrington (nephew), and Mrs. Fairhurst; Mr. James Hepherd (nephew), Miss Percival (niece), Mr. William Fletcher, Mr. and Mrs. Seymour Mead, Cogshall Hall, North.. wish; , Mr. W. D. Jeans, Alderman Bennett, Mr. H, J. Westbrook and Mr. Rumney, of Whitley. Messrs. Fletcher, Russell and Co., Ltd., were represented by Messrs. W. Russell and W. H. nudge (managing director) and Mr. A. C. Russell, of Eccles. Beautiful floral tributes were placed on the grave from Frank Fairhurst, Mini; Fair- hurst, Mrs. Cragg and Miss Smith (Southport), Mr. and Mrs. Hepherd, the directors of Messrs. Fletcher, Russell and CO., Mr. and Mrs. E. jtoughton, "Ernest, Frank and the C 01.," and Mr. and Mrs. Baskerville.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>Death of Mrs. Weir.</head>
        <p>Death of Mrs. Weir.</p>
        <p>On Saturday the death took place at Mrs. Weir, wife of Mr. T. Weir, Talbot Hotel, San- key-street, at the age of G. She died from syn- cope, having been ill for nine months. Mrs. Weir was the eldest daughter of the late Mr. William Kerfoot, formerly a farmer at Grad Sankey and later the landlord of the Nees Head Hotel, Sankey-street, and a sister a .Mr. F. Kerfoot, inspeeter for the Warrington Rural Aistriet Council. The Rey. E. C. E. Carleton efatetl at the tuwizra Wetistsday at the Cemetery,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>TRAINS "SCRATCHED."</head>
        <p>TRAINS "SCRATCHED."</p>
        <p>New Year's Arrangements By</p>
        <p>C.L.C. And L. &amp; N.-W.</p>
        <p>INCREASED FARES TO CHECK TRAFFIC.</p>
        <p>When train-faring citizens pant up the steps at the Central Station or run down. Parker- street to Bank Quay and ask for tickets to So-and-so, on Monday, they will probably be told by the harassed booking clerks that the trains have been ticked Off; for it is a fact that the number of trains running will average about half those we have been accustomed to .seeing puffing into and out of Warrington plat- forms.</p>
        <p>And when the long-awaited trains eventually steam in and bookings are made travellers will be charged 50 per cent. extra on all single fares, the return fares being double the increased single fare. It will be New Year's Day and a ease of unhappy returns. It is anticipated that the higher rates will diminish. passenger traffic commensurate with the ..• lesser train Service. Season ticket holders are stated to remain at the same figure. , The fare to Liverpool' (retutn) will be 4s. 6d., and to. Manchester, '3s. Md. The alterations in the train services are drastic, and, worse than all, the public cannot yet be apprised of all the details, for "The Warrington A.B.C. Railway Guide " cannot be put on sale for a few days, owing to the authorities not having checked the times. As soon as this is completed, however, the A.B.C. will be printed to guide and befriend. Travel- lers must provide their own philosophy, and that they will need to be philosophical may he deduced from the particulars we are about to give. • Trains are to be So infrequent that •no-risks must he run in the Catching, of them. Chesterton once remarked that the best. way to catch a train was to miss the one before it. If We do that now we shall have too long a time to Wait. •</p>
        <p>• THE C.L.C.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>Important Alteration In Morning Expresses.</head>
        <p>Important Alteration In Morning Expresses.</p>
        <p>That frequent and prompt service of trains between Manchester and Liverpool via War- rington, which has been the convenience of thousands of Warringtonians and the admira- tion, of the whole of the railway and cora- mer.cial world, is, with the New Year, to be- come a relic of the Old, and those that have gone before. Instead of hourly, the expresses will pass by (obliging by staying a couple of minutes to pick up and set down passengers at Warrington) as nearly as possible every two hours. Occasionally slow trains will come and go, and the travellers' bad temper will go on. for ever—if the war lasts so long. It was a facetious cynic who -once said that the best thing about Boston was its expresses to Philadelphia. After to-day we. cannot say the same about Warrington expresses to Man- chester and Liverpool. - Those desirous of visiting the Midlands or Yorkshire will, instead of going direct, have to reach their destination by means of what bowlers call a "round-peg"--that is, they will have to proceed by inclining to the left and going into Manchester, whenCe they may "catch a con- nection." This is due to the fact that no Mid- land or Great Central 'Railway trains are to pass through Warrington, all from these sys- tems terminating or commencing their jour- neys at Cottonopolis. For those following occupations in Manches- ter and Liverpool the expresses have been ideally comfortable and convenient, and it will be a source of satisfaction to the Warringtonian, Mancnnians to know that although the 8.55 express,will be cancelled an express will leave. at 835, and if they Miss that a' slovi' train at 8.55 will cater for their needs. It only means twenty minutes less in bed, and if that is indispensable• then one's breakfast time must be curtailed.. With eggs at prohibitive prices and bacon only within reach of millionaires and farmers, there should be no difficulty in that! The earlier popular express—that .at 7.50 is to make way for a half-fast at -7.45, stopping at all stations excepting Padgate and 'Glaze- brook. On alighting at Manchester the pas- sengers should see the great clock indicating 8.27.</p>
        <p>The 7.45 for Liverpool is being replaced by one at 7.50, which is to halt at six stations, and isi duo in at 8.35- The 8.50 non-stop is to re- main staunch and true to the scores of business men who have trusted it for many years. The day's work done in Manchester—and, perchance, that more entertaining record "The Day's Work," by Bioing, or "The' Day's News" in an evening sheet in their hands— they will find that the 4.30 express and the 4.38 slow are still running, but the 5.10 and 6 o'clock expresses have been spiked, a new train at 5.40 being substituted and scheduled to stop at Urmston on its journey to Warring- ton. After that there is a non-stop to War- rington at 6.30 and then 8.30. The last train to leave for the convenience of local patrons is 10 o'clock. "Second houses" in Manchester for Warringtonian pleasure-seekers are impos- sible without the subsequent employment of a motor car or the expense of staying at a hotel. Of course, there is always room at Manchester Police Station, but Warrington must continue to maintain its good name. The 4.30 from Liverpool is still on, as also is the 4.35 slow and another slow at 5.23, but there is no other until 6.20, when a new express will get up speed. The last train leaves at 10 o'clock—a eemi-exprese- Padgate's Misfortune.</p>
        <p>The authorities have not been Over-consid- erate to our Padgate friends, several of their trains having, been wrecked, metaphorically speaking. They cannot come to Warrington by rail after G. 59 at night, and instead of return- ing at 111.20 they will have to wait half an hour longer—in which time they can walk the .dis- tame. One looks forward to a considerable improvement in their physique. Still, it is all very sad. For years they have been using the train service as .if it were a tramcar system— and the railway company have been glad of their custom--and now several trains in which they have been pleased to travel are, like all that is human, to pass away. Sankey residents are more fortunate, 'only one or two of the really popular trains being "scratched."</p>
        <p>Very few trains will pass through the Central Station on Sunday, and none of them will be non-stop in either direction—Liverpool or Man- chester. The last train from Manchester leaves at 9.48. Visiton to Liverpool will have to say "Goodbye" about an hour earlier than that. On and after Monday all stations from Sephton to Southport (Lord-street)—both in- elneive)—will be closed. Those wishing to visit Southport will have to ,journey via Liverpool Central and Exchange (L. &amp;Y.), or via Wigan (L. &amp; N.-W. Railway).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>L. &amp; N.-W.</head>
        <p>L. &amp; N.-W.</p>
        <p>sonic Of The "Favourites" Still Running.</p>
        <p>Although many of the trains on the L. and N.-W. Railway have been "scratched," several of the old "favourites" are still to run.</p>
        <p>A highly popular Warrington-London train, for soldiers returning from furlough—that at 12 noon—is to be retained, but the 11.30 p.m. is to start at 11.9. The 8.10 a.m. for London leaves eight minutes earlier, and, instead of changing at Hartford to meet the Liverpool-Euston ex- press, passengers will be able to remain rented, RS they will be in the boat train which goes straight through from Fleetwood to the Metro-' polls. The 5.14 having gone west," a new train will be run at 4.32. The latest train for Crewe sa Saturday night will be at 17.23. Mo•re Station kaviag been closed, residents</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <p>1)</p>
        <p>rorallittellignre</p>
        <p>Lights For Coining Week. Light up O-bscure Vehicles. Lights. Saturday - 4.3* ... 5.30 Sunday Monday Tuesday Wednesday Thursday Friday Saturday Lights must remain obscured till 6.56 aim.</p>
        <p>Lord Bishop at Padgate. The Lord Bishop of Liverpool will preach at Christ Church, Padgate, to-morrow evening.</p>
        <p>Co-operative Society,. Concert:</p>
        <p>The Education Committee of the Warrington E.L. Co-operative Society, Ltd., have arranged a grand concert for Thursday evening in the Co-operative Hall.</p>
        <p>Fishing Money for the. Infirmary. Sir Gilbert Greenall,. Bait, has sent .seB 55., the receipts for. fishing tickets for 1916 to the funds of the Infirmary, in 'accordance ,with his custom.</p>
        <p>Special Services at St. Anntis Church._</p>
        <p>-Special services of Prayer, tha.nlsgiving, and resolve will .be held at St.. Ann's Church to- morrow. In the morning the Lord Bishop of Liverpool will preach..</p>
        <p>National Bread in Tins and Cobs Only.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Master Bakers announce that to effect an economy in 'labour the Natioital Bread will be manufactured in tins and -cobs only, at the price of tenpence a 41b. loaf.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>'Wycliffe P.S.A.</head>
        <p>'Wycliffe P.S.A.</p>
        <p>_ Mr. B. Farrar prEksided over the meeting of the Wycliffe P.S.A. last Sunday. when Mr. R. Jones gave an address on "Christmas: Its Festivities and True Lesson to Christian People," "Messiah" at Bold Street Church.</p>
        <p>On Sunday evening at Bold-street Wesleyan Church a ,mnsiedl service .was held, selections from the " Messiah " being rendered. The solo parts were taken by Miss Brough. Miss Ker- shaw, Miss Whitmore and Miss E. Taylor.</p>
        <p>Priory School Children's Concerts. The Soldiers' Home will benefit ta the extent of over £l6, the gross receipts at the concerts given by the children of the Priory School, St. Austin's-lane, on Wednesday and Thursday of last week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>Save</head>
        <p>Save</p>
        <p>Mr. Proud, the manager of the Savings Bank, wishes us to remind our readers that the best New Year resolution that can be formed is to save.</p>
        <p>All the money that, can he collected will be required in the near future; and we cannot do less than second Mr. Prond's admonition.</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath Reehabites.</p>
        <p>The "Pioneer " Juvenile Tent (Independent Order of Rechabites) have elected the following officers for the New Year :--Chief Ruler, Annie Ratcliffe; Past Chief Ruler, Erie Watkins; Deputy Ruler; Naricy Griggs; Levite, Gladwys Watkins; Guardian. Edna Goodliffe; and Steward, Elsie Bellamy.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>Richmond Gas Stove Co., Ltd.</head>
        <p>The Richmond Gas Stove Co., Ltd.</p>
        <p>Mr. James W. Slack, F.C.1.5., has • been appointed managing director of the Richmond Gas Stove and Meter Co., Ltd. Mr. Slack has been connected with the company for 21 years, and. has held the position of secretary for nearly 15 years. Mr. Arthur Slack, A.C.1.5., has been ap- pointed secretary to the company. Death of Mrs. Barnes.</p>
        <p>We regret to announce the death. of Mrs. Rachel Barnes, wife of Mr. Thomas Barnes, who passed away at her house, 7, Chester-road, on. the morning of Christmas Day.. • She was in her 59th• year. The funeral took place on Thursday afternoon at the Hill Cliffe burial ground. -A service at the home and-graveside was conducted by the IteN% 11..0gden, Among .the.inqurners present were Mr. Thomas Barnes (husband), Mr. Herbert Barnes (son), Mrs. Bell (daughter), Mr. i Peter' Clare (brother), Mr. and. Mrs. Gil- bert, Mr. and Mrs. Speakman, Mrs. Adams, Mrs. Ward.</p>
        <p>The funeral arrangenieuts were carried out by Messrs. J. Mounfield and Sons.</p>
        <p>AVarrington Commercial School.</p>
        <p>The first annual social and dance promoted by the students of the Warrington Commercial School, •held in the Winmarleigh Cafe on Thursday evening, was a pronounced success. Music for dancing was provided by Mr. T. Blake's band, and Mr. Sinclair was an efficient M.C. A whist drive formed part of the even- ing's enjoyment, the prizes being .presented to the winners by Mr. J.' Marsh, as follows:— Ladies: 1, Miss G. Day; 2, Miss L. Dickens; consolation, Miss E. Dearne. Gentlemen: 1, Mrs. Gleeson (playing as "gentleman"); 2, Mr. Chadwick; consolation, Sig. D.. Johnson, R.N. During the intervals games were indulged in, the prize for musical arms being won by Miss E. Hatch. As a result of the social a donation will be sent to one of the local charity organisations.</p>
        <p>Entertaining the Wounded. A first-rate coneert, arranged by Mr. Jack Hands, was given in the military ward at the Infirmary on Thursday evening. Songs were rendered in a pleasing . manner by Miss Ida. Mather, Mr. T. Attwell, and Lce.-Corpl. Cox, while Mr. Albert Wilde and Mr. W'Wainwright were loudly applauded for their concertina and piano duet. Encore after encore was given Mr. Harry Edwards, a light comedian, who rendered songs in his usual bright and breezy way. Mr. Joe Hands "brought the house down" with his comic selections. The accompanists were Mrs. L. F. Wood, Miss Blanche Mather and Mr.. T. Wainwright, Owing to the rely bad weather the weekly concert for the wounded at the Infirmary was somewhat marred. Four of the artistes, includ- ing Miss D. Truslove, Miss M. Truelove, Miss M. Hockenhull, and Mr. A. Pearce, eventually put in an appearance and entertained the soldiers, for about, an hour. Full justice Was done to all the items rendered by the artistes, Mr Donald Pearce and Mr. W. B. Randall (who arranged the concert) accompanied.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>De Bear Schools, Ltd. : Examination</head>
        <p>De Bear Schools, Ltd. : Examination</p>
        <p>Successes..</p>
        <p>At an. examination held on 24th November, under the auspices of the Nation.al Shorthand Association, the following students were suc- cessful :—Elementary Theory Alice Ellis (14), Ethel W. Leigh (15), Frederick It. Taylor (16), Amy Webster (14). Advanced Theory: Annie J. Bridson (16), Jessie Highley (16), !lefty L. Millington (14), Laura Pye (14),1da B. Wright (14). Speed: Irene M. Knowles (15), 50 words per minute. All students entered for this ex- amination passed. At an examination held on- oth December, under the auspices of the National Shorthand Association (Typists' Section), the following Btu- dents passed the junior examination :—Alice Ellis (14), Jessie Higbley (16), Irene M. Knowles (15), Hetty L. Millington (14), Laura Pye (14), Ida B. Wright (14), Amy Webster (14). All students entered for this examination. passed. Jessie llighley is bracketed first with three others (out Of over 300 candidates), while Hedy L. Millington. is placed second, being only one mark behind the firsts.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
