      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>SIR DOUGLAS HAIG</head>
        <p>SIR DOUGLAS HAIG</p>
        <p>Thanks "Examiner" Readers For Sending Xmas Puddings</p>
        <p>TO THE ARMY.</p>
        <p>"The Daily Telegraph" and "The Daily News," with whom " The Examiner" co-oper- ated in the work of raising sufficient money to provide every •soldier on active foreign service with plum pudding on Christmas Day, have re- ceived the following telegram from Sir Douglas Haig, Commander-in-Chief :</p>
        <p>The Christmas puddings provided by the</p>
        <p>kind forethought of " The Daily Telegraph " and " The Daily News " have been issued to the troops to-day, and all ranks very much appreciate the gift. We are glad tc think that people at home are thinking of us at this season.</p>
        <p>In the name of the British Armies in France and Flanders, 1 wish to thank every one who so generously subscribed to thc fund, and so enabled every officer and man to have plum pudding with his Christmas dinner. D. HAIG, Commander-ir-Chief.</p>
        <p>_ -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>FOOD PRODUCTION.</head>
        <p>FOOD PRODUCTION.</p>
        <p>Warrington In The Van Of Progress.</p>
        <p>Warrington ha s• made a great move in the way of food production, and perhaps this dtter- mination may be principally placed to the credit .of Councillor Henshall (chairman of the Small Holdifigs and Allotments Committee) and our estimable Town Clerk (Mr. J. LyOn Whittle). Councillor llenshall has caused a survey of all the unoccupied lands to be made by the Borough Surveyor, and the Town Hall, Parks and Baths Committee have placed at his dis- posal all spaces which are suitable, including the recreation grounds in Lovely-lane and Vic- toria Park. Within the ,borough bundary about 10 acres of land are available. There is a great eagerness shown to obtain allotments, and some. 60 applications have already been received. The committee undertake to provide manure, seeds and implements at cost price and on easy terms of purchase. Requests to landowners and others in the neighbourhood to place unoccupied lands at the disposal of the committee have, up to the pre- sent met with no response, and we are Assured that the committee are determined to put into force the powers they possess of taking over all such lands which are suitable for agricul- tural purposes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>CHR ISTMAS EVE AT 40 WARRINGTON WORKS.</head>
        <p>CHR ISTMAS EVE AT 40 WARRINGTON WORKS.</p>
        <p>Visiting Soldier Kisses Forty Fair Employes.</p>
        <p>A time-honoured custom at a certain works in Warrington may be little known outside local works circles, and the following descrip- tion may be of interest as an actual example last week. The idea is to celebrate the break- ing up for the Christmas holiday by a festival among shopmates during the afternoon of Christmas Eve, and each of the various de- partments of the works organises its own separate and individual festivity. The festivities vary with the kind of worker. At the works to which this relates, the pro- grammes were something as follows : The mechanics combined with. the electri- cians, in. the machine shop, at one table loaded with -pies, cakes, fruit, beer, wine, mineral waters, Iv. Their Chairman was a man of local note, being a member of the Warrington Tribunal; and on :the arifiFal of • tle—Warl4. Manager, 'accompanied by the General ,Manii- ger, on their tour of inspection, his (the Chair- man's) gift of oratory was given full swing in the toasts that followed. The two visitors had to partake warily on account of the dozen or so other departments that were expecting them for a brief visit. Before they left the 'mech- anics' festal board a flashlight photograph was taken of the group. • Next was a girls' department, and their spread consisted of • tea, cakes, sandwiches, fruit and wine. Ilealths were drunk, • and the presence of a works soldier home from the Front on leave was taken advantage of by him respectfully to kiss in turn each one of the forty or so happy girls as they sat, each kiss being prefaced by a. military salute. . Certain other less indiscriminate sentes fol- lowed under a bunch of mistletoe, thought- fully erected by other male attendants, all in the spirit of happy comradeship that attends such works' functions.</p>
        <p>The next visit was to another, largely, girls' department, where the few male workers sat with the foreman at a table apart from the two tables of girls. The same viands as last graced the festal board, but the speciality in other entertainment was mainly singing and a gramophone. The former was done without accompaniment and the good old-fashioned songs sung by sweet-voiced young girls were well appreciated.</p>
        <p>More healths were drunk, and then progress was made to another part of the works, where men and girls were about equal in number. Hero dancing was the chief help to sociability, after the usual light repast, and the mana- gerial visitors each took his choice of dainty- footed maidens and tripped the light fantastic toe with hearty enjoyment. And yet more healths were drunk, but the floor still kept in its place?</p>
        <p>At this time urgent messages came that the office staff and heads of departments were waiting for the Works Manager and the Gen- eral Manager to carve the turkeys at their special feed in the office dining-room, but there were several other Works feasts to be visited yet, so the visiting party broke np, the Works Manager taking on the balance of the works feasts and the General Manager somewhat re- luctantly going to his carving work. The remaining feasts were found to be much the same as to furniture as those described, and the spirit of happiness, camaraderie, loy- alty to the firm, and, above all, patriotism to the country, was the same also. At each of the little ceremonies mentioned tender reference was made to comrades away fighting in distant lands, and to some at rest there for ever.</p>
        <p>The offioe staff "do" was a fiimilar occasion of cheerfulness, and afterwards the desks in the main office were put on one side, and mnsic, dancing and games prevailed, with the help of the works' piano, until 8 o'clock, when the majority of the happy family looked into the General Manager's office to wish him a Merry !Christmas and then went home in -couples of assorted 'genders.</p>
        <p>Unfinished.</p>
        <p>"I'll have you understand, sir," said the 'bustling little chap, "that I am a self-made man?'</p>
        <p>"All right, old man," said Jigger. ir`Now, •rnn along home and finish up the job and then I'll talk to you."</p>
        <p>Without Accessories.</p>
        <p>The price of mistletoe is more than three times what it VMS under normal conditions, and .3 good many patriotic young people have agreed iii the interests of national economy to content idiemselves with kissing one another without accessories.—" Punch."</p>
