      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>WIDOW'S SORROW.</head>
        <p>A WIDOW'S SORROW.</p>
        <p>Three Sons And Two Sons-in-</p>
        <p>Law In Casualty Lists.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Hill, a widow, of 12, Norman-street, - is the mother of three soldier sons and two eons- in-law, who are all in the casualty lists. - The first Hon is Rifleman Robert Hill, Rifle Brigade, who has been missing since October 220: • His mother will be glad to receive news as ,to his whereabouts. He formerly worked for Messrs. Armitage and 'Rigby, and is 20 years of age. -He was, also a Member of the Latchford Cadet -Corps. Private William Hill, Lancashire' Fusiliers; is the second, and nothing has been heard of him since his mother received a letter on. October 20th, saying he was in hospital With fever. He enlisted on the outbreak of war and was previously wounded by shrapnel in Galli- poli. Educated at Silver-street School, Private Hill was formerly employed by Messrs. Rylands Bros., Ltd,. The other son is Private Joseph Hill, South Lancashire Regiment, who is in hospital suffer- ing from strained valves of the heart Like his brother, William, he enlisted on the out- break of war, and was formerly'' in the .employ: ment of Messrs. Armitage and Rigby: '</p>
        <p>Private Joseph Stacey, South Lancashire Regiment, who was reported killed in last week's issue of "The Examiner," and Private J. Bradbury, who was killed in August of this year, are the two sons-in-law.</p>
        <p>CROSSING TnE BAR.</p>
        <p>TENNTSON.</p>
        <p>Sunset and evening star,</p>
        <p>And one clear call for me! And may there be no moaning of the bar When I put out to sea.</p>
        <p>Twilight and evening bell, And after that the dark ! And may there be no sadness of farewell When I embark.</p>
        <p>For though from out our bourne of time and place ' • „The fod may bear me far,' —1 hope to see my Pilot face to face When I Dave crossed the bar.</p>
        <p>pretty well and am now lying in hospital at the base."</p>
        <p>Enlisting on the 30th October of last year, Private Bond had been in France six months. He received his education at Hamilton-street School and was' formerly employed at the 0/ford Tannery. He was a member of Wycliffe P.S.A.</p>
      </div>
