      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>THIRD WAR=eI4RISTMIIS.</head>
        <p>THE THIRD WAR=eI4RISTMIIS.</p>
        <p>FESTIVITIES AT MILITARY HOSPITALS AND PUBLIC INSTITUTIONS.</p>
        <p>Novel Efforts to Disperse the Gloom.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>ARMY PLUM PUDDINGS REACH WARRINGTON,</head>
        <p>ARMY PLUM PUDDINGS REACH WARRINGTON,</p>
        <p>As nearly as possible Christmas has been cele- brated in the usual time-honoured manner. tinabh; to cater for ,themselves, those' in. hospi- tals were specially provided for by. kindly officers and friendi, be seen from the graphic accounts which follow.</p>
        <p>At all the military hospitals the following ,elegram from the Bing was read amid cheers :</p>
        <p>"At this Christmastide the Queen and I are thinking more than ever of the sick and wounded amongst my sailors and soldiers. From our hearts we wish them strength to bear their sufferings, speedy recovery to health, a peaceful Christmas, and 'many happier New Years to come. Christmas Day, 1916."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>LORD D,EREY.</head>
        <p>LORD D,EREY.</p>
        <p>2,100 Wounded Soldiers Relish Plum Puddings.</p>
        <p>"And how did you like.the plum puddings?" a half-circle of firobesieging wounded soldiers were asked in one of the wards at the Lord. Derby Hospital, Winwick. • • The reply was immediate, conclusive, goner- ou'S and general in approval—a reply which will gratify all those who contributed to the Army Pudding Fund—" Pine!" "Beautiful!" "Never tasted better!" And quite genuine opinions, too; they were not out to pull one's leg. . • . .</p>
        <p>• At Winwiek 2,100 wounded soldiers enjoyed the puddings after a happy feast of the usual Christmas dishes, which, • however, owing to the scarcity, did not include Turkey. Gallipoli heroes sought consolation in recollecting how they nibbled Turkey, with General Sir lan Hamilton as chef.</p>
        <p>• It was a happy time that they spent. All the wards were .tastefully . decorated, some oe ,a more latish and gorgeous scale than ,others, but all had .it Christmassy appearance, For weeks the nurses and patients were engaged in making paper flowers and festoons, and not a pedestal, pillar, or wall in . the numerous wards went unadorned. Overmantels were edged with ivy and flowers/ and across the mirrors were painted in white the usual Christ- mas greetings—such as "A Merry Christrims" and "Cheer, boys, cheer "—and unusual 'but easily understood‘declarations, such as "Good luck to. all,. ikot forgetting the boys in the trenehes!" and "Welcome to the Colonials!" In the ward where the last-named imposed itself a Canadian kiurSe and Australian Soldiers had. quarters. In several places striking portraits of Lord Kitchener were -heightened • in ,interest by decorative effeets.l,L - • •</p>
        <p>In one of: the ofien.air sleeping wards the " Tommies" showed infinite humour. Over each bed sat large-anottoes, including: "Home, sweet home,"- " What is home without a mother?" and "The Lofd will provide "—" as if the nurses won't" laughed. the sister-in- charge. Hanging over some of the beds twinkled encouraging sprigs of mistletoe, and one soldier remarked "There'll be some vic- tims yet." . He was thinking of visiting days. Throughout this week whist drives have been held, and much pleasure has been derived from participation in them, handsome prizes, given. by friends. in thcl djstrict, adding to the in- terest.</p>
        <p>Like the walls and vestibules, the great theatre was specially decorated and cinema entertainments have taken place every night this week.</p>
        <p>"Tommie " ;at .Winwiek have voted ;heir ClristmáB Al.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>THELWALL HEYS.</head>
        <p>THELWALL HEYS.</p>
        <p>Vi hist, Billiards, Seasonable Fare, And</p>
        <p>‘Tisit To Theatre.</p>
        <p>The patients at the Thelwall Heys Convales- cent- Home spent a very .enjoyable Dinner consisted of turkeys (the gifts of local ladies and gentlemen), plum puddings (provided by the Army Pudding Fund), and afterwards dessert. .</p>
        <p>Presents were placed on the table for the men, all of whom received a packet of chocolate,- the gift of Messrs. Cadbury. An excellent tea was also provided, meat pies, mince pies, and trifle being included in the menu. Much amusement was caused by the finding of small articles, such as rings, in the trifle. After supper, games were indulged in. A whist drive and billiard tournament were held, Prizes being given to the successful competitors. The ,billiard tour- nament has been in progress for some weeks past, and the prizes on Christmas Day were gained by; 1, Mr. GI Hutchings; 2,Trivate Wal- lace; 3, Sapper Budd. The successful players at whist were: 1, Private 'Moody; 2, Sergeant Priest; 3, Private, 'Wood; 4, Private Broad; booby, Private Park. • On Thursday the patients Visited the Royal Court Theatre to witness a Performance of "The 'Toreador," and after Wards partook of high tea at Atkinson's Cafe. '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>COTTAGE HOMES.</head>
        <p>COTTAGE HOMES.</p>
        <p>Feasting And Entertainments Of Delightful Hue.</p>
        <p>At Padgate Cottage Home.l on Christmas Day the children. were-. .as well provided for in. the edible line as in the entertainment line. They had a special dinner;* consisting of roast beef and plum pudding, every child later being sup- plied with an orange, apple, nuts and sweets. - During the dinner the Mayor and Mtiyore-ss (Alderman and Mrs. Peacock) arrived, and upon entering the dining hall were received with loud applause. The Mayor, speaking to the children, said "My dear children,, lam very pleased to be with you once again. This is the third Christniks I have visited you. It gives me great pleasure to See you looking so bright and happy. As Mayor of Warrington, I like to feel that all children are well eared for. I hope that you will fully appreciate all that Matron and officers have den° for you, and that you will benefit in after years by the education you have received in the Homes."</p>
        <p>Responding on behalf of the officers and Guardians, who were represented by Mrs. Dr. Peacocks said: "I beg to thank the Mayor and Mayoress for coming here to-day. They have so many calls Upon their time •thrit, I am sure we fully appreciate their kindness." A "plum pudding" procession was formed, and Mr. Ellis, as : Father Christmas, greatly interested the children. • rn the evening a concert was given,. Miss E. Rigby singing and Mr. Bibby sustaining- the humorous part. The. children were later pre- sented with toys from the Christmas tree by Mrs.. Hall, who-was accompanied by Mr. .W. C. Brooks. A playlet, "Beauty and the Beast," Will; given by several of the children. alie proceedings were organised by Mrs. Law- rem" the thatroi, and the officers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>WHITECROSS.</head>
        <p>WHITECROSS.</p>
        <p>THE INFIRMARY.</p>
        <p>Matron As Santa Claus Astonishes 01(1 how The Bed .I)a.tienis Participated In Lady. -,. AVlnst Drive. \ . Special efforts were made at the _lnfirmary An old lady spending her last ailing days in the ,hospital at Whitecross on Christmas this: year to make the time of the patients as ' hapPy Eve was astonished to see hovering around as possible. The wards were most her bed the gorgeous and masculine form of 'tastefully. decorated by the nursing staff, the Santa Claus, and, -feminine modesty impelling Military Ward, thanks to the kindness of M her. into hysteria, she shouted an alarm and Messrs. Peter Walker and Son, Ltd., looking jumped from between the blankets after a particularly festive by reason of the large , poker. "I'm not going to have men in my quantity of flags and streamers: On Saturday evening a whist drive was held room in nightshirts," she exclaimed, desper- :for the military patients and nursing staff. The ately. . ' The position was so serious that Santa Claus majority of the men were in bed, but they were enabled- to take a great interest in the immediately decided on a disclosure of iden- actual games played, 'owing to the fact that titY. Peeling the hood wig and beard from numerous prizes were given to the men - who her head, Santa Claus proved none other than the: Matron (Mrs. .Loxliam), guessed nearest to the highest or lowest scores. • glowing - with ' . Chairman of the triumph at the amazement she had caused. On Christmas Day the Board (Mr. J. IL Smethurst), accompanied by Everyone bubbled over ,with joy, ,as Santa . Claus affectionately tticked the sudden-riser the .resident doctors, Dr. and Mrs. Seager, Dr. into. bed again. . Even ~the ~ old lady quaked .: and Mrs. Hodgkinson, Dr. Binns, Mr. Henry Collin, Miss Seeger, Master and Miss with laughter as she adjusted her nightcap and settled . her head ,On the pillow. "God Smethurst, distributed "toys, 'chocolate and use- ful garments to the children in the civil wards, bless you," she said; "but you did give me a fright. Ha! ha! ha!" , . , and in the female wards, useful articles of clothing and chocolates. In the male ward, On Christmas Day Santa Claus was again in pipes, possession, this time in. the person of the tobacco, cigarettes, useful articles ofclothing, de., were handed out. In the military Assistant Master (Mr. IL H. Sewell).. As tea ward the gifts were of a special kind, and was concluding lie sprang from nowhere on to consisted of pocket-books, purses, and soldiers' the stage, telling them that they were all knives. having a good time—a thing they knew, of During the - morning the Mayor - visited the course—wishing them the best of luck, and so Institution. . on, including funny stories and the heartiest' The Christmas dinner was served at twelve good cheer. Mr. Sewell was made up so well i o'clock, the cdrvers being Mr. J. H. Smetturst, that hardly any recognised him. : , Mr. Henry Collin, Dr. Seeger, Dr. Shaw, and And then in the evening he was an the ramp Mr. W. Daintith. again. Coming into the large, dining hall , In the evening an entertainment was pro- (beautifully decorated) with . a suddenness . vided by. the nursing -staff, and the festivities Which was almost as astonishing 'as the .event , were continued on Wednesday evening, on on the previous evening when the Matron ap- Thursday (when the Whitecross Troupe gave, peered, he advanced across the room, straight an entertainment), and on Friday. Last Sunday to ()flea the oldest'smallest and liveliest ladies Mr. and Mrs. F. Short provided a high-class in the institution, and, liaving circled - her entertainment. waist, they executed a waltz to a measure con- . The whole of the expenses of the extra fare sonant with age and good humour. ; and presents were provided out of the special This Christmas there, were 428 inmates in fund which is annually raised by the Chairman the workhouse to cater for, their fil-r! being, of the Board (Mr. J. H. Smethurst) and he is 4001 b. of beef, 6001 b. of potatoes, 3001 b. of plum , most grateful to the subscribers for their kind- puddings, 2001 b. of cake, sweets, nuts, fruit, , flees, particularly to the management of the and tobacco. Beer was served to those with a , Queen's Picture House and the Grand Electric taste for it and mineral waters to others. ; Theatre, Bridge Foot,' for their kindness in Among those present when' dinner' was: allowing collections to be taken• in their re- served were the Mayor and Mayoress (Alderman: spective places of amusement. and Mrs. Peacock); Master Peacock, Mr. W. , Furness, Mr. A. BottOmley (clerk), Mr. W. Loxliam (aster),. Mr. W. C. Brooks and Mrs, Hall (Guardians), the Revs. E. Downham• (act- ing as chaplain for the Rev. W. Bracecamp), IL Ogden (Nonconformist chaplain), Dr. Pea- cocke (district medical officer), Mr. Jonathan Wood and Mr. J. Willett.</p>
        <p>Mr. Furness, in welcoming the Mayor and Mayoress,,, expressed Abe pleasure. it afforded him.. to gee the: inmates; 'and .he hoped they would spend a very happy Christmas. •It was satisfactory to find that the wbole of,. the arrangements were so complete. (Applause.) , The Mayor 'said thiS ;was his. fourth annual visit in his official capacity, and every time he visited the institution .he saw that various improvements had been carried out. It was comfortable and well managed, and in case of necessity be did- not consider it would be a greit hardship to be ~an inmate, especially under the mastership of Mr. Loxham. (Hear, hear, and applause.) Thanks were due. to him and the staff for their care of the in- mates, and he was sure it was much appre- ciated.. He hoped all present would have a good time, that next year would be a better one than this had been, and that when they met again on a similar occasion peace would have been declared. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>SOLDIERS.</head>
        <p>THE SOLDIERS.</p>
        <p>"In the trenches last Christmas; in the Workhouse this," was a sententious expression by one of the 222 wounded soldiers being looked aftei. at 99, Guardian-street. But they were all happy. The wards were resplendent with. evergreens, paver flowers and mottoes, the general appearance savouring of Christmas as much as the 24 turkeys which were cooked for the ." Tommies" welfare. At the Christmas Day meal they also chased three hares, two brace of pheasants, and a, goose which waddles no more. The potatoes dished out to the boys weighed 3001 b. and the plum puddings 2001 b.-- made in this institution: The Army. Plum Pudding Fund's contribution was received after the others had been made, and will, conse- quently, be served on New Year's Day. , More joy! ' • '</p>
        <p>Grapes, nuts, oranges, &amp;e., Completed the menu. •</p>
        <p>In the evening there was a whist drive, and later a concert of a very enjoyable character. Following the concert there was dancing until midnight, the nurses and officials participating It may be added that the evergreens were supplied by Sir Gilbert GreenalL Bart., C.V.0., Mr. J. C. Parr, Mr. W. Per Rylands, Mr. F. A. Frost, Mr. C. E. Parker and Mrs. Broadbent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>RADDON COURT.</head>
        <p>RADDON COURT.</p>
        <p>A Varied Round Of Festivities.</p>
        <p>The patients decorated their wards, und these were judged on Saturday by Mrs. Dillon:, Miss Peggy Broadbent and Miss Margaret Harvey. Prizes were offered by Miss Monks, Commandant of Cheshire (4.8),, and were presented during a concert on Saturday evening by Mrs. Dillon to the Wards best decorated. • 'The concert was pleasing in its variety, several children performing very charmingly. The special feature was the einite unexpected pres- entation of Christmas presents by the patients to the Vice-President and Commandant, the Sisters, and a large eake to the Nurses. On Christmas Day a sumptuous dinner was served. The Chaplain, Rev. G. C. Briggs, came in during the meal to give his good wishes and to present a card to each man. Mrs. Charlton Parr distributed presents from a prettily deco- rated Christmas Tree, and the time-honoured. figure of Father Christmas appeared and handed the parcels to Mrs. Parr for presentation: Then followed games and old-fashioned . country dances.</p>
        <p>On Boxing Day a whist drive was provided for the patients and a number of Red Cross nurses from the local detachments.</p>
        <p>On Thursday all were given tickets for the matinee at the theatre, this being made possible by the kind gifts of money from several friends. Priday the patients were entertained by Mr. Rigby and his.school,children with a charm- ing Concert.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>Needlework Guild Parcels.</head>
        <p>Needlework Guild Parcels.</p>
        <p>The Police Court Missionary JtMr. W. Laws) desires to. acknowledge a parcel a cloth- ing from.. the Warrington :Needlework _Guild, per Mrs. James, Fairclough. The Committee ,of, the Warrington Citizens' Guild of Help beg to acknowledge, with many thanks, the reeeipt of a parcel .of.elothes ,frem the Weet Vieehire Neediest* Guild.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>MUTINY VETERAN.</head>
        <p>MUTINY VETERAN.</p>
        <p>"Hard Head" Who Fought In 26 Engagements.</p>
        <p>DEATH OF MR. T. YUILE.</p>
        <p>Warrington lost another of its Indian Mutiny veterans, on Tuesday morning, when the death took place of Mr. Thomas Yuile, at the residence of his daughter, Mrs,. Smith, 71, Oldham-street, Latchford.</p>
        <p>Mr. Yuile, who had attained the age of 81 , years; was born in Glasgow, and in his early days was employed as a cabinet maker. He en- listed in the 3rd Bombay European Regiment, which became known aS the "Hard Heads," because of the hardships it subsequently had to endure. He was sent to India with the Honourable- East India Company, and after a while was ordered from Poonah, along with his regiment, to endeavour to quell the mutiny. He took part in no fewer than twenty-six engage- Ments, but came safely through them all with- out a wound. Of course, he had many narrow escapes, one of the fiercest .engagements being at Jhansi, where no fewer than two hundred and fifty of his Comrades were killed. ,He was the proud possessor of a medal and clasps. Mr. Yuile came to Warrington in 1860, was • married at Grappenhall Church on June sth, '</p>
        <p>1862, and celebrated his golden wedding about three years ago. .Until about ten or eleven years ago he was emploYed by Messrs. Tinsley, tan- ners. He leaves alfamily of threesons'and two daughters:, one .son, Mr. William Yuile, , who Inkre enlisting lived ,with:his wife and children at 112, Wellington=street,sbeing at present at the Front. •</p>
        <p>The funeral, of a semi-military character, will take place at the Warrington Cemetery at three o'clock this (Saturday) afternoon.</p>
        <p>, •:.;t.,Ak'..NziA:N.. .... . . ..t.,M.;M....5.14,.(k.5&amp;w%&lt;......?...5..,.fi.:.,.,,:.. .. ,YL,,i4s,''..N.,,\..%.•Aig...tufAbv.•-•.••,,•':•••• . : aN:';,4%,.4,.\,.•....:,,:1%.:.*Ne.10::::.:.;:-.:.:....... ~..,,‘ ... . , ,1t.:KVh'..:.:'..:.....:.......:,.:4...,,,it5..:.;:.,:v..•k..:-....'.,. " :, •N'"W:‘,.., %...,..:.:.,i,•::A..,...:....,.. .. 2, 4,.::::,....:-.:,1:::::..;....,...??:.,....... , N*i?:.'ittr, ,›M.,:?:,.:..,,;... , .........,..,,, , '.•'. 5it.0.;N..:E:...k;',.:',:.4,'.'..:1''. •,:..... • . - .::.I::::.:.. *N .s.:-.'.. ..U.?.e;1N.,'.''',*...•'.. ". -k-... .... j. k‘ikt.s.,. R,,'''''.::.,,,,N5....ik'ir.;:5.;'.::;,:.. '.... : , ' •. : :..t.t:fi4N4§i:M:,'Z::•:::. WV-,,%'\'. .NK.'5:'......:....2.,:g;,,,,'.'.i.i .:.:.•. 1:,..., ..1: ',:tati:: ".11.:,ii ,s ..'::. ..,..q.:!''. tatti:s. s':. :::::::;;....;::::.•...::' j.::‘,. \•&lt;A, n:tql:.: ... ,s ',VA., .::&lt;.:: ' ..,' . '.. *... .. \:"'s,•.: :-.t";:i.:ft.,, k -..'.:'*:*'-' s'.:t.' ..!',l.:i. :.:s:.S.s.:s.'':*::3e..- ' ' ... '''..)..,. • isk,,,eN ''.;;N;a:.ai,,,:2, ~,,Ri•i‘4,l'ks \ \',:-.:.''''....si...:::'''' ''' . t, NAttiO , ~,;,, ,k,,,...„.:::;,,,,,,,....::.::::::&gt;,.... ~. • ~.,..i..,, ...,....%,,,,;.„..,0%., . v..., \,,,,4,.*p. .....ix;,.•., . '.tx:,:w•,'6.").•‘N.,,s ..V.4'Pt.,.'i...c.5.••'...-.''.'ikolft' ''''. A5:453'.%.,N,‘',. ~,,,..::•••,:..,•-:::,-; '• ~- .... .•,,,,.: ' - .4,.,,,:0vt.c....m,5,,..,, ..,„.,;......, , ... . . . %.;,;.:,:.:.,...,..,....,,,,,,wz,;(•,• . ... • .. -•::: ... .. ,„: .:1:!.k. :.„..,:,...4%.,., ...:,........,\5r:.;:,,,,,,t:,.i,,,,:.,..:t.,i:..,, • .. ..„,. it., :,,,,,.. le '' . • i , ... .1 - ' ...... ~ ...... . ..</p>
        <p>THE LATE THOMAS YUILE. Served in the Mutiny. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>RYLANDS BROS., LTD.</head>
        <p>RYLANDS BROS., LTD.</p>
        <p>Soldiers Of Labour Entertain</p>
        <p>Wounded Soldiers.</p>
        <p>The festive season. of ,Christmastide was not allowed to pass over by those having the admin- istration of Bylands Bros.' War Relief Fund without showing special attention towards the inmates of the different Military hospitals. , The headquarters of the Recreation Club presented a very animated scene On Wednesday evening, when a hundred Soldier guests sat down to a sumptuous knife-and-fork tea, most creditably served by the manageress.. Mrs. Wilde, assisted by several ladies divthe office. After tea an adjournment was made -to the billiard-room, where, following a little opening speech by Mr. G. E. Knott (chairman of the NS ar Fund Committee), an excellent concert was held.</p>
        <p>The Chairman welcomed the soldier guests, and .explained at some length the objects of the fund. The total number of -Wives at present re- ceiving. ss. ..weekly from this fund is 162, and since the outbreak of war the sum of between £6,500 and £l,OOO has been 'subscribed and paid out by the firm and employes. The concert followed, and each item was cor- dially appreciated. Special. thanks are due to the artistes: Messrs. T. Humphreys, J. Sephton, G. Gough, W. Moores, J. Wrench, H. Edwards, G. Wilde, and Sergt. Leadbeatter. Mr. A. Prow was an able accompanist. While the patients were partaking of refresh- ments before leaving, Sergt.-Major Halsford pro- posed a vote of thanks to the employes of the firm for the kindly way' in which the men had been received, Mr.- Haigh responded on behalf of the committee.</p>
        <p>The patients present from the different hospi- tals were as follows :—Lord Derby's, 40; White- cross, 20; Raddon Court, 35; and Infirmary, 5.</p>
        <p>. _ The best thanks of the committee have been expressed to Mr. W. IL Phiflip, who arranged' the concert, and to Mr J. Critchley, hon. secre- tary'of the War Relief Fund, who did not spare time o rtrouble in working out the various de- tails 'of the entertainment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>RICHMOND'S.</head>
        <p>RICHMOND'S.</p>
        <p>Annual Christmas Dinner Enjoyed By Employes.</p>
        <p>The members of the Richmond's Employes' Club held their second .annual Christmas din- ner in the works' meesroom, Grappenhall, on Thursday of last week. About 120 of the mem- bers were present, and the: President (Mr. J. A. Ransome) took the chair. An excellent dinner was provided and a most enjoyable evening 'spent. Songs were, given by Miss Elsie Hewitt, Mr. Edwards; ''Private Lloyd (an old employe home on leave. from the Front), and Messrs. Brookes, Hands, 'Connor and Sergeant. Mr. W. Pemberton wae. the pianist for the evening.</p>
        <p>The -various "toasts" were well received, and, replying to that of "-The success of the Firm," the Chairman (Mr. Ransome) referred to the objects for which the club has been formed. He hoped this was only the beginning, .and in course of time that it would be possible to further„ develop a scheme to provide the workpeople with the means whereby they Might enjoy" their leisure time in healthy re- creation, lie believed he was correct in stating that the employes of Richmond's, through the medium of this fund, were doing what no other firm in.' t.l* town had undertaken. The welfare of each of the employes who had en- listed was considered. This Chrittmas, assisted by a special subscription of £7 10s. from the Employes' Club, it had been possible to send out comforts to about 250 of their workmates who were on service, and addresses were still coming in. Over of:90 had been granted in cash to assist 'soldiers and their dependents and to help discharged soldiers, and until such 6111139 as their pensions had been granted.. The reL serve fund was now over X2OO. Mr. W. Caddick, supported by Mr. F.. Garm- ston, suitably acknowledged on behalf of the men.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>Great Circus at Liverpool Olympia.</head>
        <p>Great Circus at Liverpool Olympia.</p>
        <p>The great circus at the Olympia, Liverpool, is proving an immense success, it being fell of novelties. Owing to the enormous length of programme the managcsnent , have decided to. present only two performances each day at 2 and 7. One of the special attractions provided is the full band of the Grenadier guards,</p>
        <p>Lli. _11:41_ _ _,..___l___ ~ _________A</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>ORFORD BARRACKS.</head>
        <p>ORFORD BARRACKS.</p>
        <p>Christmas Fare AVith Unbeaten Record.</p>
        <p>Although the company at Orford Barracks was not so numerous on Monday as at Christ- mas, 1915, the annual festivities were carried out .with the utmost success. Last .year over six iiundred- sat down to table, when the event ,was • organised by Lieutenant quick (then Regimental-Sergeant-Major), and a: fine concert and dance followed. This year the number was much smaller, but the management was in very able hands—Sergeant Leadbetter, assisted by Sergeant Brenan—and the result proved entirely sOsfactory. Over 70 inembers of the Expeditionhry Corps—most of whom had been wounded early in the war and all belong- ing to the South Lancashire Regiment, gathered round the festive board. The arrangements were perfect; so much so, indeed, that nearly a dozen splendid turkeys and geese, hams and legs of pork were left untouched, together with quite a big array of plum cakes and other attractive fare: There was no scarcity. Take this Christmas menu and see if it could be improved on anywhere in the kingdom: Roast Turkey, Roast Goose. Roast Legg of Pork. Ham. Roast Beef. Christmas Pudding. Vegetables ad lib. Cheese, nuts and various comestibles.</p>
        <p>And there was rum sauce with the pudding! There were eight Christmas. puddings, each weighing 2.51 b. and costing as many shillings. Dinner was served in the Barrack Rooms, which were, beautifully decorated for the occa- sion,': as well as the Gymnasium, in which the concert and (lancing subsequently took place. Al! the flags of .the Allies were prominently displayed, and cordons of ' streamers and trophies, with. 'plants and other decorative material were lavishly used in order to make the scene as enhancing as possible. While the men were at dinner the popular: Commanding Officer, Colonel Moggridge, en- tered the room and was greeted with a round of cheering. He was accompanied by. Major Upperton, Captain Stott, the Quartermaster, Captain Crone and Acting-Adjutant Chambers. It should here be stated that while the cater- ing was looked after by Sergeant Leadbefter, the details of placing the goods in a delectable manner on the tables was entrusted, and with the most cemmenciable results, into the hands of the Arn4 cooks, marshalled by the Quarter- master and Quartermaster-Sergeant O'Brien. When the feast had reached its climax glasses were filled, and, on the call of Colonel Moggridge, three cheers were given for His Majesty the King. Colonel Moggridge, , in a characteristic little speech, wished all present a merry Christmas. He hoped that at that time next year they ! would enjoy the festival under better condi- tions. . He hoped that they would then Spend Christmas in their homes—the happiest experi- ence Of all. He then proposed the health of "The Boys at the ;Front," a sentiment which aroused the utmost enthusiasm. The cheering„ had scarcely died away when a stentorian voice ! called for a" round" for Colonel Moggridge and the officers of the depot, and the response I was deafening. At six O'clock a very enjoyable concert,. arranged by Sergeant Leadbetter and a num- ber of civilian friends, began and lasted for, two hours. The Gymnasium afforded seating., accommodation .for close on four hundred per- sons, all the soldiers bringing their friends for the occasion. Smokes and -refreshments- were- layishly served during the entertainment ' and the dance which followed. Miss M. Carson, of Manchester; specially came for the occasion and recited a stirring Piece dealing with an incident of the present war—" The Hell 'Gates of Soissons." Songs were rendered , by Messrs. Macgregor, Harry Edwards, T. ' Humphreys, J. Wrench, J. Hands and W. Carter; while Corporal Van Rousden (Welsh Regiment) delighted everyone with his his- trionic contributions. All these friends were heartily applauded. Mr. A. Wilde and Mr. Wainwright were the instrumentalists. Danc- ing began at 8 o'clock (the floor of the Gym- nasium having first been cleared for the, fes- tivities) and continued until midnight, everyone present expressing their highest ap- preciation of the arrangements for their enjoy- ment. Mr. Sheppard (violinist) a,nd Mr. Wilde (coneertinist) added to the piano. effect.-Three cheers were given for Sergi:int I.k:adbetter at the, close, this popular non-eom.,. assisted by Sergeant Brenan, Corporal Stamp and riarice Corporal Jones, having worked like Trojans. during the evening.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>CROFT ORGAN.</head>
        <p>CROFT ORGAN.</p>
        <p>Dedication At Christ Church By The Bishop Of Liverpool.</p>
        <p>MUSIC IN VILLAGES.</p>
        <p>In spite of the very unpropitious weather quite a large congregation assembled to take part in the ceremony of dedication of the new organ by the Bishop of Liverpool at Christ Church, Croft, on Saturday. The Rector (the Rev. H. I. Wadeson) officiated as Bishop's Chaplain, and also intoned the service, which was shortened evensong. The Rev. J. T. Owen, of Winwiek, read the lessons. The sermon was preached by the Bishop. Mr. T. Hurdus, organist and choirmaster, presided at the organ, and after the dedication ceremony and sermon an organ recital was given by Mr. F. M. Darby, organist of .the Warrington Parish Church.</p>
        <p>The offertory was for the new organ fund and amounted to Xi 4. _</p>
        <p>The organ is a splendid three-manual instru- ment and has been built by Mr. Alf. Holt, of Manchester and Rochdale. The case is of the best figured oak, with carving and ornamenta- tibn to match the general interior fittings of the church. The front pipes had been beau- tifully and artistically decorated by the Rector. Altogether, the organ is of very fine appearance, while the rich and melodious tone, with almost infinite seope for the expres- sion of all that is best in devotional music, leaven little to be desired. Mr. Derby's pro- gramme had been most admirably arranged for showing the capabilities of the n'ew instru- ment, and his skilful renderings were greatly admired by the congregation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>DISCORDANT GERMANY.</head>
        <p>DISCORDANT GERMANY.</p>
        <p>The Bishop tot* as his 10`xt, "And it came to pass when the minstrel played that the hand of the Lord came upon him." Of all the musical instruments we possess, the organ is supreme in the rendering and bringing out all that is most sublime in the music of devo- tional worship, he said. Some of the oldest of the congregation might carry their minds back to the time when the instrumental part of the worship in our village ehnrches was rendered by the more musical portion of the parish- ioners--one playing the flute, another the vio- lin, another the oboe, and so on. There was much to be said for this form of instrumental , music as an aid to divine worship, but the organ, the king of instruments, as most were willing to agree, was infinitely better. "Your organ," added the Bishop, "has been dedi- cated at a marvellous time—during the great- est war ever waged in the world's history and at a period when the war cloud was terrible in its aspect—but he .hail • faith to believe thit light was now penetrating the dark cloud, and that the end of the terrible struggle was draw- ing to a close, with victory as the reward of our efforts for humanity's alike, _and the up- lifting of the weaker nations of the earth. "There could possibly be no peace on the enemy's terms. We had seen too much of Ger- many's perfidy and broken pledges under the most sacred of treaties. We had faith in God to believe that for humanity's sake and for the future peace of the world the arrogant military power of the enemy would be broken far all time. The dedication of that organ had taken place at another marked period of time—the eve of Christmas—and this of itself should inspire us with hope for the future. The Rector had told him that they had al- ready subscribed sufficient money to pay for its oast. "The new instrument would help to make the services more congregational and more de- votional. The highest conceptions of our greatest musical composers could, by the organ, be most beautifully pourtrayed, and our hearts and minds be uplifted, and heaven brought nearer to them and they nearer to heaven. The noblest ideals of our poets, our hymn writers, and the true meaning of words could be best brought out by the mystic power of music. The best wish he could have for those at Croft was that the blessing of the Almighty be continually upon them in their worship, and might the strains of their beau- tiful organ always awaken in them a desire to worship God in spirit,and in truth." On Sunday afternoon the Organist (Mr. T. Rurdus) gave an organ recital. Each item was specially selected to bring out the distinctive features of the new instrument. ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>ANNUAL TEA PARTY AND SOIREE.</head>
        <p>ANNUAL TEA PARTY AND SOIREE.</p>
        <p>There was a large gathering of parishioners and friends, Young and old, on Boxing Day.</p>
        <p>After a hearty tea the usual time-honoured :4 Christmas games were indulged in by the If jurettil ; afterwards dancing and music. An ,enjoyable time waa spent by all. The proceeds will be devoted to the Sunday School Prize Fund.</p>
        <p>DOMESTIC' SERVANTS. Splendid opportunities are offered in Quebec. Many well-to-do families, in town and country, are glad to er.gs.ger servants from the Old Country, and are constantly en the look-out for them. General Servants, Housemaids and Cooks are in great demand and are paid good wages.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>QUE EC</head>
        <p>QUE EC</p>
        <p>(CANADA). • The climate is the healthiest known; the home life is Ideal; and the people happy and prosperous. Further particulars from LI.-Col. P. PNLLETIER (Agen t- Gene al for Quebec), BE, Kingsway, London, W.C. N. •</p>
