      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>TTIE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, tECEMBER 30, 1916.</head>
        <p>TTIE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, tECEMBER 30, 1916.</p>
        <p>-in: roses should not fail to adopt either of tho nbuve plans.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>z a Amtm 7.1j7 • : '.P.1741.1.- 1157 P T fstil 11-dif • -•-.</head>
        <p>z a Amtm 7.1j7 • : '.P.1741.1.- 1157 P T fstil 11-dif • -•-.</p>
        <p>I.4YMM</p>
        <p>LOCAL FACTS AND FANCIES.</p>
        <p>By CRITIC.</p>
        <p>Phew: Clariitmaki ()Ter. What a relief :</p>
        <p>"I " sigh. " You 7 wonder why</p>
        <p>But you can find satisfaction in the old pro- verb, " What tho eye doesn't see, the heart doeen't griovo."</p>
        <p>Maye you had a good time, or just a quiet, war-time one?</p>
        <p>Some have and some haven't.</p>
        <p>Which derived the best benefit I will leave to your own judgment. /</p>
        <p>Works opened last Wednesday morning, and though most of the workers had made good their vacation, they rolled up in fairly creditable numbers.</p>
        <p>By the way, I believe they are to make merry again this week-end. •</p>
        <p>Ono of the works closed down yesterday at 4 p.m., and will re-open on Tuesday morning.</p>
        <p>They commenced their holiday with a party, and at down, as .the guests of their employer, to a hearty repast in the Primitive aathodist Schoolroom last night.</p>
        <p>After which the usual routine of merry-mak- ng was continued.</p>
        <p>The employes of another firm, I believe, are to be the guests of their employer in the New- field Mission Room on Monday evening.</p>
        <p>This being New Year's Day, they will no doubt make some good resolutions.</p>
        <p>But those who made the resolution to dine not freely but wisely earlier on,, will, I think, be under a great temptation.</p>
        <p>What matter so long as they keep their reso- lutions regarding the making of Munitions?</p>
        <p>Perhaps he most trying time of all this Christmas was to be seen in the Post Office.</p>
        <p>One member of the staff whom I happen to know—fairly well—never knew where he was above two minutes at a, time.</p>
        <p>Everything wee dealt with most satisfactorily. I am informed .that very few letters had to, be sent to the dead letter office through being in- sufficiently addressed.</p>
        <p>We are evidently at last learning to address our letters to the satisfaction of the Post Office people.</p>
        <p>The usual Christmas Day services were held on Christmai; Day morning—one at the Parish Church and the other at the Primitive Metho- dist Church..</p>
        <p>And were attended. I believe, by fairly large congregations.</p>
        <p>Several tea parties have taken place during the past week in the Primitive Methodist Schoolroom.</p>
        <p>A friend of mine recently described this schoolroom as the home of tea parties and socials.</p>
        <p>I hear there is to be a special service at the Wesleyan Chapel to-morrow evening.</p>
        <p>What it really is I won't disclose, but I can assure you that there is a treat in store.</p>
        <p>Amongst the numerous popular songsters who have promised to attend (I am confidentially in- formed) is Miss Ada McHugh.</p>
        <p>I have no need to tell you who she is, for all lovers of good singing know.</p>
        <p>This is a fact, not a fancy. There is much depression in the village duo to the total sus- pension of the train service on Sundays. That complete Sabbath calm will be manifest as a result is considered too great a sacrifice. Lymni does not like the idea of being isolated as if it were a plague spot.</p>
        <p>Perhaps the Urban District Council will press for at least one train up and down on a Sunday It is a matter upon which they should be agreed. On this occasion the bone of contention which has been picked enough, might very well be thrown out of the carriage window,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>PRIVATE WILLIAM TAYLOR.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE WILLIAM TAYLOR.</p>
        <p>Twelve Months Since He Left Blighty,</p>
        <p>Seems Like Twelve Years.</p>
        <p>Writing home to Moston-street, Newfields, Private Wm. Taylor, a member of the Army Cycle Corps, who is at present in Mesopotamia, says: "Just a line in answer to the one which you posted .on October 2nd, and which I re- received to-day, November 4th. I was so pleased to get a letter, for it is very diaappointing when the mails come in and you are not fortunate enough to, get one, but when you do happen to drop lucky it fair bucks you up. Ido not know why you upset yourself so much when you did not hear from me, for when I left Egypt I told you that it would be some time befote you heard from me again. Anyhow, lam glad that you have received one at last. Someone must have been mistaken when they thought they saw me in France, for I have not yet been there, and don't want to go. It is just about twelve months to-day since we left dear old 'Blighty,' but it seems about twelve years.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>Lymm Lads.</head>
        <p>Lymm Lads.</p>
        <p>"I am glad you heard about my being forth- nate enough in dropping on some Lymm lads. I am with Jack Ravenscroft and Fred Read (both Newfield soldiers), and I hope to remain with them for the duration of the war. By the time you receive this letter I suppose it will be well-nigh Christmas. I cannot tell you what sort of a time we shall get out here, but I do hope that it will be a little better than last time. I hope that you all will halo a jolly godd time at home. I know you can't have the same time as you would if we were all at home, but you must try to forget and have a good time.'"</p>
        <p>In a further letter received later in the week the gallant soldier says: "Ton say in your last letter that it will not be long now before you see me again. I sincerely hope so, but Ido not know how long it will be. I am sorry to tall you that Jack Ra.venscroft has been moved; bad luck, by he used to come to see me every day: Never mind, there is still quite a good 'nUrabar of tymm lads left. There are Fred Read, Jack Johnson,' Sidney 'Whitfield, Jack Broody and Philip Bell from Rush Green. I am sorry I cannot send you a Christmas card, but I enclose you a drawing which has been clone by one of the boys in my.tent. I will, now draw to a conclusion, wishing you all a merry Christmas and a prosperous New Year,"</p>
        <p>Private Wm. Taylor is a well-known local lad. Tie enlisted in the Rifle Brigade% soon after OA commencement of war at the age of seveneen, afterwards getting transferred to the Army Cyclist Corps, with which he went to Egypt about 12 months ago. Strange to relate, at the time that he met these Lymm lads he was on duty at tho docks helping to unload the very boat by which they had travelled. We will not attempt to describe the meeting, but we will leave it to the reader's imagination,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>CHRISTMAS.</head>
        <p>CHRISTMAS.</p>
        <p>LI:);1 The Festival Was Observed In This District.</p>
        <p>Although most people decided to celebrate this year's festive season in as quiet a way as pos- sible owing to the war, signs of the usual revelry could easily be detected throughout the,,village last week-end.' Christmas Eve, which witnessed sharp frost, was mostly spent indoors, and as. the casual visitor walked through the village he could easily detect, through the peals of laugh- ter that came floating through the air from the numerous .cottages, that the spirit of Christm'as still abounded, though on a small scale. Those who were lucky enough to have their sailor relatives preseneat their family gatherings tried hard (and very rightly) to forget the gloom that is at-present upon our country, and gave their heroes a good time. Those who were parted unavoidably from their loved ones also tried hard to forget and did their best to make those trave boys who were amongst us as •happy as possible. We can truly say that on th-Cwhole they had quite a good time. Those who were not fortunate enough to have their friends with them will find consolation in the fact/that at most of the training camps in England their boys were well looked after by the military authorities. For instance, one well-known " Tommy " who is at present train- ing at th'e Kimnel Park Camp, through the medium of a letter states that on Christmas Day their menu consisted of roast pork, with season- able stuffing, Christmas pudding, nuts, sweets, chocolates, fruit; in fact, everything that the inner man could desire, and we know that the boys "out there" got their usual pudding, some of them as the guests of "The Examiner" readers.</p>
        <p>The usual custom ,of carolling was still con- tinued, and the employes of one well known munition firm formed a choir and spent their Christmas Eve carolling on behalf of the Red Cross. Their efforts, we Understand, were highly successful, and they have been able to hand over a substantial sum to this most de- serving charity as a Christmas present to the wounded " Tommies." At Brookfiold.</p>
        <p>The wounded " Tommies " who are at present recuperating at our local hospital had, we under- stand; throuc,h the ingenuity of several local ladies and gentlemen, coupled with the untiring efforts of the splendid hospital staff, quite a merry time. The celebrations commenced on Christmas Eve and were continued on Boxing Day. The programme consisted of several con- certs, special Christmas fare, and, on a small scale, a fancy dress ball. One of the wounded heroes, speaking to our representative about th9ir Christmas dinner, stated that they ate so much at dinner that when supper was placed on the table in the evening they simply had to refuse.</p>
        <p>Last Minute Rush at the Post Office.</p>
        <p>At the Post Office animated scenes were wit- nessed. Although the pressure was:not so great as in former years, the staff were greatly handi- capped through the wretched weather. One of the most surprising things in .this sphere was the great last minute rush. Up to Friday evening business was practically normal, and the staff were looking forward to a comparatively easy time, but they were doomed to dis- appointment; for on Friday evening the collec- tions suddenly began to boom, and by Saturday it was found necessary to clear the boxes at regular intervals. This meant finding extra temporary labour, which, at the present time,, is exceedingly difficult, but thanks to the tact and excellent supervision of Mr. James Arnold, our efficient postmaster, and the excellent assist- ance rendered by the women who so readily volunteered their services, the rush was gradu- ally overcome, and by noon on Boxing Day they had the satisfaction of seeing everything cleared. Tea was provided on Saturday evening For the entire temporary and regular staff, and break- fast on Christmas Day morning, and this duty was brilliantly executed by Mr. and Mrs. Geo. Crags, of Bridgewater-street. Strange to relate, the grett last minute rush was due entirely to the working classes, but this may be greatly attributed to the fact that they are mostly muni- tioners, and through working late every evening in. the week they would not have time to post their Christmas cards mail Saturday afternoon.</p>
        <p>Ist CHESIIIIRE VOLUNTEER REGT.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>No. 1 (Lymm) Platoon, "D" Company.</head>
        <p>No. 1 (Lymm) Platoon, "D" Company.</p>
        <p>PLATOON ORDERS.</p>
        <p>Tuesday, January 2nd.-7.30 p.m.; Recruits' Drill; 8 p.m., Physical Drill. Thiirsdetiy, January 4th.-7.30 p.m.. Recruits' Drill; 8 p.m., Platoon Drill. Friday.-7.30 p.m., Musketry Instruction (Recruits). Orderlies.—Sergeant Moston, Privates Moston and Wilson.</p>
        <p>G. R. WIIALIAINISON, Lieut</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>PRIMITIVE METHODIST SUNDAY</head>
        <p>PRIMITIVE METHODIST SUNDAY</p>
        <p>SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>Annual Christmas Treats.</p>
        <p>The annual Christmas treats in connection with the Primitive Methodist Sunday Schools took place on Boxing Day and Wednesday evenings respectively. As in former years the Boxing Day treat was made public and the usual presentation of prizes took place. The evening's entertainment opened with a tea pro- vided by the. ladies of the church, under the brilliant supervision of Mrs. G. L. Welford and Miss F. Shore. The large schoolroom was not big enough to seat all who came for tea, and it was found necessary to have a second sitting. After tea the scholars who had earned prizes were handed their treasures by Mr. G. L. Welfords who took the chair. A special feature of the presentation of prizes was the handing over of the prizes and certificates won by the scholars in the annual Scripture examination. Miss L. Priestner came first with 90 marks out of a possible 100. Master N. Priestner was second with 80 marks, and Master W. Sharpies secured the third with 79 marks.</p>
        <p>A brilliant concert by the Children:and friends had been organised by Mrs. G. L. Welford and -Miss F. Shore. At the conclusion of the chil- dren's part of the programme, a cantata, en- -titled "Jesus of Nazareth," was rendered by thwelder scholars, in which some very no sing- ing was obtained. Solos and • duets *ere ren- dered by the Misses L. Appleton, E. Tonge, L. Priestner, M. Evans, E. Warham, and Messrs. C. Wilkinson, C. Ravenscroft, T. Sherratt,and Geo. Cross.</p>
        <p>At the conclusion of the programme the_Chair. 'man (Mr. G. L. Welford) expressed sympathy with Mrs. Priestner and Mr. Edwards, who were 'unable to attend through sickness. On Wednesday evening the scholars held their own private treat, at which good number were present. After tea the evening was devoted to games, which were keenly enjoyed by the children.</p>
        <p>Lirtiment for Father.</p>
        <p>Eatle Boy: "A penny'orth eaell of liniment and , liquid cement, please." Chemist: "Are they both for the same. per- son? Or shall I wrap them in separate pack- ages?"</p>
        <p>• -Little Boy: "Well, I dunno.. Muvver's broke 'er teapot, so she wants.tho Oement; but farver wants. the linimmt. 'E's what tiauvver broke 'er teapot on."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>Wesley Guild enjciyable social evening took place in the Wesleyan Schoolroom on Boxing Day evening, those in attendance being, for the most part, members of the Guild. Games were heartily entered into, refreshments being served during an interval.</p>
        <p>" Messiah " at th- Wesleyan Church.—Special "Messiah" services were held on Sunday in the Wesleyan Church. Mr. Henry Brocklehurst presided in the afternoon, while Mr. T. Deakin took the chair in the evening. There were good congregations. A rare treat was provided by Mr. Richard Tranter (baritone), of St. Helens, who sang "Thus saith the Lord " and "Who may abide," "Behold darkness," "Why do the nations?" and "Behold I tell.,you a mystery," each item being splendidly rendered. Mr. W. Tynan- contributed in beautiful style "Comfort ye" and "Every valley" and "Thou didst not leave." Mrs. J. A. Fairclough (sop- rano) gave most effectively "There were shep- herds," "Rejoice greatly," "How beautiful are the feet," and "I know that my Redeemer liveth." Miss Miriam Webb sang very sweetly "Come unto Him," whilst Miss May Webb feelingly gave "He shall feed Hi 's flock" and "He was despised." Miss Edith Hughes took the recit. and aria "-Behold a virgin" and "0 Thou that tellest." The choir, which was con- siderably augmented, rendered the choruses "And the glory," "For unto us," "Glory to God," "Behold the Lamb of God," "All we like sheep," "Lift up your heads," "Halle- lujah," and "Worthy is the Lamb." Mr. F. E. Penketh conducted. Mr. A. Prow was an efficient accompanist. Special collections were taken on behalf of the trust funds.</p>
        <p>Treat to Soldiers' Childrett.—As a result of the generosity of several ladies and 'gentlemen in the district the third annual tea, entertain- Merit,. and Christmas tree for those children in the parish, whose father or brothers are away serving in H.M. Forces, was held on Thursday evening in the Schoolroom, when about 200 children spent a very happy time. About fifty mothers also attended and. enjoyed them- selves. As previously, the treat was onganised by St. Thomas' Band of R9pe workers, under the leadership of Mr. Joh/VP:allows, who never tires in his efforts to make the children happy. After a hearty tea the children' were handed presents from the Christmas tree by Mrs. James Richardson (Walton) and were after- wards entertained by Mr. White in several con- juring tricks. Votes of thanks were heartily accorded Mrs. Richardson, Mr. White, the donors, and the workers. Rev. E. W. Clarke, in the absence of the Vicar, addressed the children. Each child was given a mince pie and orange on leaving for home. Donations were kindly given by the following :—Lady Banner, Mesdames Lyon, Armitage, Richard- son, Cooper, Partington, Parr, V. Davies, Ran- some, S. Owen, Milling and Parkinson; Messrs. L. Davies, P. Caldwell, T. Baxter and the Vicar. Cakes, &amp;c., were given by Mesdames Hatton, .Stafford, J. Hallows, Pittam, Seed, Berry, Whitelegge, Spencer, Davenport, Thompson and Higharn; the Misses Thatcher, Appleton and Kenwright. All the bread was giten by the ViTarringtciii Co-operative 'Society.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>PENKETH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>Death of Mrs. Ada Walker.—Mrs. Ada Walker, wife of Mr. T. Walker, for many years a resident in Penketh, passed away on Sunday last after a very short illness. A chill contracted developed into double pneumonia, which left no hope. Two years ago they made their home in Newsham Park, Liverpool. Mr. T. Walker at the present time is a private in the sth King's Liverpool Regiment.</p>
        <p>Military Intelligence.—Dispatch Rider George Fowles, son of Mr. and Mrs. William Fowles, Cuerdley, is home on furlough from the Wes- tern Front, where his experiences began about eighteen months ago. Originally a Sapper in the Royal Engineers, he was later on selected for 4ispatch work, which he has zealously car- ried out. Two other brothers are at present serving in the R.F.A. Privates J. Molyneux, Victor Owen and Oliver Gibbons have just recently crossed the Channel.</p>
        <p>Priv4,te Prank Wheeler, on of Mr. Richard Wheeler, Station-road, is home on furlough for the first time since the; outbreak of war. As a regular Grenadier Gpardsman he was soon in the fighting line, doing his share dur- ing the retreat from Mons. Wounded twice, he has eventually got through safely, with a long list of fighting to his credit, a fitting climax to his thirteen years service.</p>
        <p>Wedding.—On Saturday Afternoon, at St. Mary's Church, Sankey, the wedding of Mr. Ed. Williams, son of the late Mr. E. Williams and Mrs. Williams, of Birkenhead, and Miss Annie Finch, second daughter of Mr. and Mrs. Finch, Station-road, Penketh, was solemnized, the Rev: J. R. Joneeofficiating. The bride, who wore a navy blue costume trimmed with ermine, and hat to match, and carried a bou- quet of lilies and chrysanthemums, was given away by her brother, Mr. Frank Finch. The two bridesmaids—the Misses Alice and Minnie Finch (sisters of the bride)—wore grey whip- cord dresses a,nd black hats, and 'carried bou- quets of pink chrysanthematits-(' Mr. Walter Robinson; of Birkenhead, acted as hest man. The service was choral, Miss Standish officiat- ing at the. organ. A large 'number of -useful nd' valuable presents have been received.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>GREAT SANKEY.</head>
        <p>GREAT SANKEY.</p>
        <p>Nurse Waiters Leaving Sanatorium.--Having been successful in obtaining an appointment as tuberculosis nurse -under the Wigan Corpora- tion, Nurse Elsie Walters is leaving Sankey Sanatorium.thiS'week-end, after a stay of about eighteen months, during which she has been very popular. Before coming to Sankey she was at hospitals at Keighley and Birthingham. All will wish her continued success-at her new post.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>WOOLSTON.</head>
        <p>WOOLSTON.</p>
        <p>• Social.—A whist drive and da4e, in aid of the local soldiers' comfort fund; took place in the NVoolston Church of England Schoolroom on Boxing Day. Twenty tables.were occupied with the whist, a record number for the village, the prize-winners being :---Gentlenien : ,1; Mr. W. Houghton; 2, Mr. J. Gatley. Ladies: 1, Mrs. IL Stafford; 2, Miss Gilfedder. Hidden numbers: 4, Mr. Ashley; 2, Mrs. J. Monks. The refresh, ments Were presided over by Mrs. Hindley, assisted by Mrs. Ryder. Mrs. ;Young and Mrs. Lythgoe. gr: W. Wainwright provided the music for dancing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>WINTEP. PARSLEY.</head>
        <p>WINTEP. PARSLEY.</p>
        <p>In very severe winters there is generally a great scarcity of parsley, more esiicially where no pains are taken to protect it in frames. The weather is not altogether the cause, the chief reason being, I think, that the present day strains have been so much improved by 'the beautifully curled leafage that its power to re- sist frost is considerably lessened, the tiny cups formed by the upturned edges holding the water, the leafage consequently being always wet, and thus liable to perish when the cold is very severe. For garnishing, of course, the beautifully curled strains are in demand, but the old kind is to my mind just as good for flavouring, and much hardier. Where winter parsley is in demand the bed should always be in a dry sheltered spot, as then the plants are sure to survive and come in just when wanted. At the foot of a sunny wall is a good place, or along a fence, dryness and plenty of air being Il important.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>MANURE SICK SOIL.</head>
        <p>MANURE SICK SOIL.</p>
        <p>Ground can get sick of manure even when heavy crops of yegetables are taken off it. Espe- cially is this likely to happen when farmyard manure is only used. Far better is it to give a dressing of .freshly slaked lime, spreading it evenly over the surface, and forking it in just before cropping the ground, doing this, say, every third year. Lime not or}ly lightens the soil, but destroys many of the slugs, grubs, &amp;c., that are found in the soil that has been heavily manured for several years. Some amateurs take a deal of persuading as to the value of manure, the idea prevailing that there is nothing like plenty of rich food where the cultivation of vegetables is concerned.—Wicx in "Farm and Home."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
