        <p>Driver Royston I 11.—Driver Royston, people's warden of Padgate Church, who has been in training since August last, and who returned to his home for the Christmas holiday on the 21st inst., is now laid up with a bad attack of brion- chitis. Dr. Anderson is in attendance. From inquiry we are glad to say that Driver Royston is somewhat better, although it is not at all likely that he will return to his military duties for some time to come. There is qyite an epi- demic of influenza colds in the parish, whole households being attacked with this complaint.</p>
        <p>Carol Service.—A special carol service was held in the Wesleyan Church on Sunday even- ing last, the preacher being the Rev. J. Glover, a Risley. The carols "In a rianger laid so lowly," " The shepherds to their flocks," and Jude's "All' hail the power" were sung by the choir. Miss Elsie Hewitt gave effective renderings of "Come unto Him" and "I know that my Redeemer liveth." Mrs. W. J. Bate sang very sweetly "He shall feed His flock." Mr. W. H. Ward presided at the organ. There was a good congregation and the service throughout was very enjoyable.</p>
        <p>Wesleyan Carol Singers.—The carol singers of the Wesleyan Church have agaim given their services in aid of the National Children's Home and Orphanage, and though the weather has been most unfavourable they ventured out on four evenings to raise funds for this worthy institution. The amount raised is now ..t9 .16s. Bd., which is considerably lower than last year, so it has been decided to continue the carol singing during this week-end in the districts which have not been visited owing to the inclement weather, so that last year's total of ..€ll ss. may be equalled.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>FEARNHEAD.</head>
        <p>FEARNHEAD.</p>
        <p>Tea Party and Concert. —The annual tea party and concert in connection with the Risley Independent Methodists was held in the School- room on Christmas Day. Owing to the rise in the price of foodstuffs, the charge for the tea and entertainment had to be raised, but, despite this fact, the attendance was a record one. The entertainment consisted of four dialogues and a humorous sketch, entitled "Oh, aint I bad?"' whilst numerous recitations and solos were very ably rendered by scholars and friends. The meeting was presided over by Mr. Llewellyn, the proceeds being in aid of Church Fu'nds.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>EX-REFORMATORY BOY</head>
        <p>EX-REFORMATORY BOY</p>
        <p>Steals £4 10s. From "Penny Bazaar."</p>
        <p>Yesterday in the Borough Police Court a youth, bearing the sonorous title of Percy Baden Powell Smith, was charged with having on the 31st October feloniously -broken into the lock- up shop of Messrs. Marks and Spencer (The Penny Bazaar), 39, Bridge-street, and stolen a purse containing one sovereign, two ten shilling Treasury notes, and about ..t2 10s. in silver, amounting in all to ..R4 10s., the property of Norah Marshall, 44, Norris-street. Accused admitted the charge. Chief Constable Nicholls said the youth—who was 16 years of age a couple of days previously —had been an inmate of the Reformatory School at Birkdale. He had apparently broken away from the school, and, as stated, broke into this shop of Messrs. Marks and Spencer and took this money. This was not the second 'or third shop breaking expedition the prisoner had been on. He (the Chief Constable) had written to the Horn. Secretary suggesting- that it was a case which should be dealt with under, the Bortal treatment, and the Home Secretary had given his consent to that course. At that time the prisoner was not amenable for the treat- ment because he was under the age of 16 years, and the present application was that he should be remanded for a week and committed to the prison at Liverpool. The • Governor at the prison at Liverpool would say whether he thought him a suitable case for the Borstal treatment, and if he was he (the Chief Constable) would bring him again before the magistrates and ask them to send him forward for trial. An order was made to this effect.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>ME LATCHFOPkD CADET CORPS.</head>
        <p>ME LATCHFOPkD CADET CORPS.</p>
        <p>Orders for week ending Januar; stlx : Parades.</p>
        <p>Tuesday, at 7.34l—Company and Physical Drill.</p>
        <p>Thursday.—Band Practice at 8 pan. prompt. Band to parade under orders of Corporal F. Nelson. Friday; at 7.3o.—Musketry. Orderliee.—La.nce-Corporal •N. Garlick and Cadet W. Skerrat. All Cadets to parade in drill order. By .order, (Capt.) J. B. S'PARKET, Officer Commanding. NOTE.—There is no football club connected with this Corps.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>FARM AND GARDEA.</head>
        <p>FARM AND GARDEA.</p>
        <p>ROAD SWEEPINGS AS 'MANURE.</p>
        <p>In the neighbourhood of towns large quanti- ties of road sweepings are taken by local market gardeners and farmers under the idea that the sweepings contain sufficient fertilising sub- stances to repay the small sum charged for them and the cost of transport and application. The Board of Agriculture for Scotland have recently received inquiries as to thafialue of such sub- stances as a manure, and for the guidance of farmers the Board have published a note on the subject.</p>
        <p>It is pointed out that the manurial value of road sweepings is naturally very variable. If they are obtained from the well-paved streets of towns and are swept up by hand they may, in some measure, approach stable manure in value. Even with town sweepings, however, there is a possibility of the inclusion of much other waste material, which may augment or decrease the manurial value of the whole.</p>
        <p>In sweepings collected from country roads there may be a large proportion of sand and other material of no direct value. On the main thoroughfares the value of sweepings has been adversely affected by the introduction of motor vehicles. This deterioration need not, however, be very great, as the amount of oil and other substances in the sweepings is generally insuffi- cient to make any appreciable difference. The smaller proportion of urine in road sweepings makes them less valuable than stable manure, as the nitrogen is not so readily available. From the comparison of an analysis made by the consulting chemist of the Royal Agricul- tural Society of a sample of road Sweepings and an analysis of horse manure, it is concluded that the scrapings are little if at all richer than ordinary soil as far as manurial value is concerned.</p>
        <p>Generally speaking, therefore, while read sweepings may be of advantage in improving the mechanical condition of stiff, badly aerated soils, or in giving .more cohesion to very light soils, it is questiona.blo whether their fertilising value, particularly in the case of scrapings from canntry roads, is sufficient to give them much monetary worth.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>RUINED FARMERS' FUND.</head>
        <p>RUINED FARMERS' FUND.</p>
        <p>Farmers' gift sale committees have forwarded to the Agricultural Relief of Allies Fund dur- ing the first three weeks of December a sum of £B,lOO. • The largest cheque was one for £3,363 from the county of Lincoln, while Berkshire sent MAO, and other considerable amounts were contributed from Cheshire, Wiltshire, Devonshire, Leicestershire and from Argentina. The fund gives practical farming help to stricken agriculturists in the invaded regions of Allied countries.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>IN THE GARDEN.</head>
        <p>IN THE GARDEN.</p>
        <p>DEEP CULTIVATION.</p>
        <p>The benefit of this is very noticeable during a dry season. If one compares the crop from ground that has been dug only one spit deep, and that from a quarter that has been trenched, say, two spits deep and well manurexl, the ad- vantage is all' in favour of the latter treatment. As there is now much vacant land in the kitchen garden, trenching should receive attention. when the weather is favourable. Many people when trenching make the mistake of bringing the . inert, crude soil to the suriace, the result }wag that the crops are far from satisfactory. A far better plan is to leave the poor soil where it is, well loosening it with a broad tined fork, uncl adding a good dressing of manure, putting the surface soil from the next trench ea to this. In this way the young plants gat a good start, the roots finding their way down to the larder that has been provided for them, and should a dry time follow they feel no ill effects ef the want of moisture. A word of wanting is, how- ever, necessary, and that is that ground so treated Should not the first year carry any tap- rooted vegetables, as beet, parsnips or carrots, the roots of such always coming forked and of but little value.</p>
        <p>ROSES OVER GALVANISED ARCHES.</p>
        <p>Many readers no doubt will be plaiting some of the many rambler roses now to be had. As a rule the supports for these are galvanised wire arches. Complaints are all too common as to such roses failing' when so grown. This failure is in the main due to the shoots coming into contact with the wire, the chemical used in the galvanising being the direct cause. To prevent loss from this cause a good remedy is to give the arch two good coats of the best white- lead paint: This answers well, and prevents any ill-effects. Last summer, when visiting the garden of a friend, I was struck with a plan that be had adopted., He had inserted lathe of wood at regular intervals over the arch, and had painted these. These laths prevented the young shoots coming into contact with the wire. Those, therefore, who are contemplating plant-.</p>
      </div>
