        <p>NORTHERN UNION.</p>
        <p>drawn game would have been a more correct result on the general run of play. As it was, Warrington were victorious by five points to nil, this being the net result of seventy minutes' play; a penalty goal from the foot of Jolley and a try by young Jones. Man for man they were much faster than Warrington, and if their pass- ing had been better timed they would no doubt have won.</p>
        <p>(Lancashire Section.)</p>
        <p>One has to go back a good number of years to find the last occasion on which St. Helens Roos. visited our' ocal nclosure, and if one's memory serves correctly, it was in the latter days of Rugby Unionism, that is, just prior to North- ern Unionism. They have got together a young and clever side, and ghould do well when normal times come round again.</p>
        <p>In this match Cunliffe was back again amongst the home forwards, and his presence put a bit of ginger into the rest of the pack. The home back division was very weak, and it is obvious to anyone that they lack finish. From neither wings can one expect a feast of brilliance, and in this section of the team we are feeling the effects of the war more than in any ether.</p>
        <p>We have oome to look upon Jolley and Tranter as the veterans in the back division, and, truth to tell, we should be in a sorry plight without them, particularly the latter. They, along with Ounliffe, were the outstanding men on the War- rington side. •</p>
        <p>The holiday matchee; witnessed the la.rgest "gates" of the season, Christmas Day ,claim- ing 3,500 spectators and Boxing Day 3,000. The proceeds of the two days amounted to £llO.</p>
