      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>LEIGH WIN AT WILDERS• POOL. –</head>
        <p>LEIGH WIN AT WILDERS• POOL. -</p>
        <p>The Christmastide programme suggested great things for the Warrington followers, but, owing to the bad weather experienced during the pro- cedi,og week, the heavy going was all against fa 4 and clever football, 'and as a'result we saw pletity of mud-larking. Happily, the rain, anew, hail, and all the rest of the choice:variety to which we have been treated lately, kept away while the game proceeded.</p>
        <p>Is usual, the hardy annual—for such it has beenme—between Warrington and Leigh was &amp;tight out with the old-time vigour, and though la* of the old faces were missing from both aides the game never lacked that local rivalry which has become a feature of these meetings.</p>
        <p>Is one watched the match last Monday ones thoughts went back to the days of the Fish, Isherwood, Dickinson and Harris combination, as representing Warrington, and the klolyenux, Dundiavin, Bennett ..and Johnson .quartet of Leigh, and in comparison one easily concluded thaitt the teams operating in this match were only a'ehadow .of their _illustrious progenitors.</p>
        <p>Still; one must give credit where it is glue, and considering the hoary ground and the pace set at the beginning by the competing teams, the game attained to a very fair standard.</p>
        <p>The visitors were certainly the superior side. eiecliting many fine rounds of pase.ing, .and Refer missing an opportunity of getting their hands to the ball. In this department of the ganke they were streets ahead of the home teaun Ganley has worked the team together splendidly, anci the experience he has brought away with him from Huddersfield is proving very useful to the yoUng Mather Lamers. You could hear 'hiin on several occasions calling to his men to "pick the ball up ". instead or dribbling. He has evidently learnt the value of being able to hanflle a ball, for nothing wins Rugby games ~ easier than sharp, crisp passing and re-passing.</p>
        <p>The movement which led up to the Second try by O'Neill was an object lesson to the home team" in thia—Plinse of the game, and nothing finer has been seen at Wilderspool this season. Commencing on the visitors' twenty-five line, the ball went from hand to hand with graceful. precision until finally O'Neill received on the half-way line.</p>
        <p>The defence by this thus was absolutely beaten, and, after a smart .sprint, the try was duly recorded. In this particular, Jolley weak- ened the defence by following up his own kick, which had altogether too much length in it to enable him to get up in time to tackle his man. To chase this sort of kick is only courting dis- aster. •</p>
        <p>Coming to the Warrington side, after leaking line allowance for the weak team turned out, one has to admit they were only moderate. Even in the forwards. we were well beaten—a thing we are not accustomed to—and the absence of Harmer, Green and Cunlifie was very marked.</p>
        <p>By the way, we were pleased to sea Fearnl,y out again, and hope that he has completely got rid of his ailment. The piok of Vas home tattoo were Jolley, Tranter, Fearnley and Jones.</p>
        <p>On Boxing Day the Rees. from St. Helens visited Wilderspool and .put up a very good show. They were quite equal to the home team ix every department of the game, and Perhaps a</p>
        <p>,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>CHRISTMAS DAY DEFEAT.</head>
        <p>CHRISTMAS DAY DEFEAT.</p>
        <p>Leigh appeared at Wilderspool on Christmas Day before the biggest Warrington " gate " of the season, fully 4,000 being present, and the result of a first-class match was a win for them by the narrow margin of two points. The first half began and ended by Jolley, the home back, who played la grand game through- out, scoring goals from penalty kicks. Between those two events Leigh had most of the play, their threequarter line, Grundy, Keegan, Car- ter and O'Neill, passing with telling precision. So strongly, however, did Warrington defend, that Leigh broke through only once, and then had O'Neill been stopped for a glaring knock- on the try would not have ensued. As it was, Winstanley received the pass and scored near the posts, and Ganley added the goal. Thus the score at half-time was: Leigh, one goal one try (5 points); Warrington, two goals (4 points). The Leigh Pia. Yes had .allowed the motor to go away with their • boots, and turned out in Warrington boots for the first half. They were allowed to change into their own clean boots for the second half. The result was soon felt. In the first few minutes O'Neill and then Davies sprinted over for tries, leaving the raudbound Warrington players spectators. Ganley and Winstanley respectively missed the goals. In the last stages Jones scored for Warrington, and Jolley added the goal. Final: Leigh, one goal three tries (11 points); Warrington, three goals one try (9 points). Teams:—</p>
        <p>Warrington: Bask, B. Jolley; threeguarters, Tranter, Potter, Horroeks and Brookes; half- backs, Daintith and Jones; forwards, Fearhley, ,Chisholm, Wall, Dumbell, Jones and Matthews.</p>
        <p>Leigh: Back, Ciarkesen; threequarters, Grandy, Keegan, Carter and O'Neill, half- backs, Mooney and Ganley; ferwards, Marsh, Winstanley, Boardman, Shaw, Getikeridge and Davies.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>REVELATION EY,.LEIGH.</head>
        <p>REVELATION EY,.LEIGH.</p>
        <p>Leigh played the better mans throughout, their threequarters being partioularly smart en the ball, and giving the best exhibition of oom- bined passing seen at Wilderspeol this year. O'Neill was especially notieeable, and it was in bringing his brilliant evil te nought that Jolley showed the stuß of which he is made when at his best. The visitors did most of the pressing in the first half, but Warrington were suffieiently aggressive te give a keenness to the eontest that is" utmally absent from holiday cline-ulster.</p>
        <p>The home Jeans was kaudicapped by having three ef the but ferwarde away---Cunliffe, Grem and Harmer. Oil if the vacancies was filled by Fecal%ley, who made his debut for this WAIN.</p>
        <p>Cualiffe staid from. rara 'working, tall Mangier has jaiited up.</p>
