      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, DECEMBER 30, 1916.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, DECEMBER 30, 1916.</p>
        <p>had them for visitors at Wildersvool on Bank Holiday and levied, another two points at their expense. The day was fine and there was again a very good "gate," the Mayor of Warrington (Alderman Peter Peacock) being one of the in- terested occupants of the grand stand. • The Bees. ,won the toss and played with the sun behind them, Fearnley kicking off. Their forwards were the first on the attack. Headed by Prescott, they raided the home half until they reached Jolley, where the Warrington full- back saved the situation. The Recs. were drib- bling nicely past the centre line when they were penalised for off-side, and Prescott's fine effort was nullified. Brookes broke through in a rush and Tranter received his pass, but d,opped the ball, and a promising move was bpoilt. Warrington were pressing when Jones was obatrueted, and Jolley, taking the kick, scored a beautiful goal six minutes from the start. Another passing movement between Brookes and Tranter were intercepted by Glover who dribbled the ball well inside the home half, Jolley being lucky to save. The Recs.' backs immediately distinguished themselves by a rare passing movement, which carried the visitors to near the Warrington line. Grundy, how- ever, missed the ball from Derbyshire. Play was again transferred to the Rees.'ohalf, and was of the keen order on both sides. licComas was off-side, and Jolley was given another chance to ,soore, but the ball fell short. War- rington dribbled up the field. Potter attempted a solo effort, but Grundy tackled him. McComas then went away, but Jolley brought him down, and a fine dribble by the Warrington forwards, led by Wail, was ruined by Fearnley getting off-side. Greenall made a good endeavour, and Jolley pushed him into touch. McComas missed a goal by only a few inches from a kick awarded for off-side. Glover beat all opponents except Jolley, who kicked away to save. The Recs: pressed and should have scored from 90111 e use- ful work by Greenall, but three of his colleagues mulled on the line, and from the ensuing serum Warrington cleared serongly. As half-time ap- proached the visitors were having all the play. Simms got over, but missed the 'try by seconds. Half-time: Warrington, one goal (2 points); St. Helens Rees., nil. - The sesond half was dull and featureless, both sides depending too much on " rushing " rather than passing. Immediately on resuming Hard- man dropped a long kick, the first mistake he had made, but the danger was cleared. War- rington continued to press, a free kick for ob- struction giving the visitors a measure of relief. Jones, the Warrington half-back, scored a try for Warrington, and Jolley made rather a bad' miss on trying to add the goal points. Result: Warrington, one goal one try (5 points); St. Helens Rees., nil. Thams:—</p>
        <p>Warrington: Back, Jolley; threequarter backs, Brookes, Tranter, Potter and Griffiths; half-backs, Jones and Callen; forwards, Fearn- ley, Cunliffe, Matthews, Dumbell; Wall and Smith.</p>
        <p>St. Helens Recs.: Back, Hardman; tbrisequar- ter backs, Glover, Derbyshire, Grundy and McComas; half-backs, Mart and Greenall; for- wards, Downey. Simms, Gartland, Hull, Parr and Prescott.</p>
        <p>Referee, MY. F. •Ifills, Oldham.</p>
        <p>morning, and' here again there 3ppeared to have been some misunderstanding, as at the time arranged for the kick-off the Cadets were not able to raise a full team, and it was agreed to concede the points and play a friendly game.</p>
        <p>The ITighfield Juniors made tlie journey to the Park to play nowley United, but owing to the inclement weather only four of the home team put in an appearance, but the referee declared the ground unfit for Play. The visitors idid not like this decision, owing to the failure of the United players to turn up.</p>
        <p>The game between the Orford Juniors and the Widnes Juniors, on the King and •Queen ground, was postponed for similar reasons and arranged for Boxing Day, on the Tanhouee- lane ground.</p>
        <p>The Howley secretary had made arrange- ments to play their replayed League fixture with Appleton' Hornets on Boxing Day, but owing to the latter being unable to raise a team the game had to be cancelled.</p>
        <p>High&amp;ld Juniors and St. Elphin's played their postponed fixture on Boxing Day, on the Tannery ground, before a record crowd of spectators. The pkoceeds from the "gate," devoted to charity, amounted to nearly Ns- The game was strenuously contested from be- ginning to end, and at the call of time the Juniors were successful; by three goals against one. This being the second defeat of the "Saints" of the seasim caused gieat satisfac- tion amongst the Juniors' supporters.</p>
        <p>All credit is due to the Juniors for their performance, as. on the day's play they were undoubtedly the better team and fully detserved their victory, Htighei, Bell, Tomlinson, Horton and Wishlade doing exceptionally well for the home team, while Pendlebur3r, in goal, pro- mised well to fill a position which has been a weak place in the Juniors' team throughout the season. Boyle, Furness and Watson were the most conspicuous of the visitors' team, the former s2oring their only point with a fine shot from the touch-line.</p>
        <p>The Secretary has received information from Mr. R. Simpson, the _chairman of the League, that he will 'have to relinquish his position in the League, as he has been notified to join IlTis Majesty's Forces on January. Ist. ) Owing to military calls the Penketh Club .will be considerably weakened, as three of their most consistent players in Pearson, Wat- son and Moss are called up to report them- selves on January Ist.</p>
        <p>WARRIfs.GTON AND DISTRICT JUNIOR LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>GOALS. P. W. L. D. P. A. P. St. Elphin's *Orford Juniors *Howley United Latchford Recs. Penketh. Juniors Appleton Hornets *Highfield Juniors Grappenhall Beweey A.S *Latchford* Cadets Widnes St. Mary's *I-Widnes Juniors</p>
        <p>*Howley United and *tWidnes Juniors have each had points deducted for playing ineligible players. *Orford Juniors, *Highfield Juniors and *Latchford Cadets have each had points added to their total.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>TEAM AGAINST SWINTON.</head>
        <p>TEAM AGAINST SWINTON.</p>
        <p>Warrington will visit Swinton to-day, and will select their team from :—Jolley; Horrocks, Pot- ter, Tranter, Brookes, Griffiths; Daintith, Jones, Douglas; Harmer, Green, Cunliffe, Matthews. Jones, Dumbell, Wall, Smith and Chisholm. The kick-off is timed for 2.45.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>RUNCORN F;E.RE ON MONDAY.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN F;E.RE ON MONDAY.</p>
        <p>Selections will be mace Irom the above players on Monday, when Runcorn are due at Wilders- pool. The match is bound to be a good one, for Runcorn are bringing a strong side, including several Widnes and Wigan players. Kick-off 2.45.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>THE 'TOWN HAVE VARIED EXPERIENCES.</p>
        <p>On Saturday the Town Club opened their Christimas holiday fixtures with a visit to Atherton. They were not able to play a fully representative team; and after a keen and hard struggle they had to acknowledge defeat by the narrow margin of one goal against none.</p>
        <p>On Christmas Day they opened their second series of the Combination fixtures with a visit to'Altrincham, and again on this occasion were not able to muster a full team. On arriving on the Altrincham ground the home club pro- vided them with substitutes to complete the eleven. Victor' .rested with the Town team by three goals against one, and one of the chief factors in this result was the splendid efforts of one of the borrowed players, who scored two capital goals.</p>
        <p>The return fixture was played on Boxing Day, on the Widerspool ground, but, owing to the late arrival of the visitors, what pro- mised to be a very good crowd of epectators gradually dwindled away, and when at last they put in an appearance there was only a poor attendance to witness the kick-off, which was one and a half hours behind the adver- tised time.</p>
        <p>After the opening preliminaries of the game, which* were very evenly contested, the home team eettled more quickly to their game, and in fifteen minutes goals had been scored by Ispinall and Woodhouse. After these reverses play was more in favour of the visitors, but they were not strong enough seriously to trouble the home defence, and a few minutes before the interval Aspinall added another goal.</p>
        <p>The second Krtion was opened very briskly by the home team, and after several futile attempts had been made, Aepinall again set the scoring machinery in motion, and Wood- house quickly added another point. Then fol. lowed a keen attack on the home goal, but it was of only, a brief duration, and the hems for- wards again transferred play to the opposite end, when Siddall scored a fine goal. From now to the close of play the game was chiefly in the visitors' half and it was only on rare occasions that the home goal area was visited and further goals were added by Whitfield and Siddall before the call of time.</p>
        <p>The final result was eight dear goals in fa-iour of the home temp. This was the first time the Warrington team have extracted both points from Altrincham ,when the latter have been visitors to the Town ground.</p>
        <p>Owing to the absence of Leah, a trial was given to Rodgers, of Earlestown, who gave a creditable aisplay.</p>
        <p>Millward has not yet' sufficiently recovered from his illness to resume his place in the team.</p>
        <p>The Christmas holiday matches have )1.- suited in the Town team gaining four points out of a possible six and scoring eleven goals against two.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>JUNIOFI NOTES.</head>
        <p>JUNIOFI NOTES.</p>
        <p>• Saturday last was another unfortunate day for the Juniors, as out of the five matches arranged there was only one played. The con- tinuous fall of rain and snow during the morn- ing had rendered the grounds in Victoria Park quite unfit to play upon, and much disappoint- ment was caused owing to the games having to be postponed. The representatives of the Recs. and the Cadets agreed to play on Christmas</p>
        <p>L••• • •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION.</p>
        <p>ATHERTON v. WARRINGTON TOWN.</p>
        <p>Warrington Town journeyed to Atherton. on Saturday, and were beaten by one goal to nil. They started the game with nine men, and in a blinding snowstorm, but .notwithstanding this fact it was well on in the second half before Simms, the Everton forward, gave the home team the lead. The game throughout was very vigorous, and if the Town had had their full team on duty, they must have won. Result: Atherton, 1 goal; Warrington Town, nil.</p>
        <p>ALTRINCHAM v. WARRINGTON TOWN. .Taxi-cabs took the Town team—or at least a part of it—to Altrineham on Christmas after- noon. The visitors started 'with eight men, and ,not only managed to keep Altrincham out, but scored a goal, when the secretary arrived with another player whom. he had come across in Lymm. TWO others Were picked out of the spec, tators, and in this manner a full team, was raised. Great credit is therefore due to War- rington on their.'victory,of three goals to one, young Woodhouse claiming two of them. Result: Warrington Town, three goals; Altrincham, one goal.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON TOWN v. ALTRINCHAM.</p>
        <p>The return fixture between the above clube in the second series of. the' Combination fix- tures was commenced on Boxing Day, one and a half hours behind the advertised time for kicking off, and owing to the late arrival of the visitors a great number of spectators had to have their money refunded or tickets given to them for another match. The follow- ing players turned out:— Warrington Town,: Kite; Rodgers, Stephens; Payne, WhitAeld, Owen; Siddall, Illidge, kspinall, Woodhouse and Banner. A I tri nc ha m Peareon ; Baldwin, Hartley; Connor, Siddall, Brogden; .Wheatman, Bee- wick, Foulds, Rowbotham and Daniels.</p>
        <p>The visitors iron the toss and decided to defend the town goal. Aepinall set the ball 'rolling and the game started at a very fast pace and was evenly eontested, each defence being quickly tested and proved to be safe. Then followed keen play in mid-field, which resulted in the home team takilig the often- give. After several shots had been successfully , dealt with Aspinall registered the first goal after seven minuted play. The visitors played more strenuously and made several attacks on the home goal and Foulds tested Kite with a splendid low shot, which was safely nego- tiated, and play was transferred to the visi- tors' end by good combination of the home forwards, and Woodhouee registered the second goal. After Ms •a, good spell of ,even play was witnessed in mid-field, neither team gaining mach -advantage: -Then the visitors made sev- eral attempts to lower the home eolo'urs, but found the defence very safe, and as the inter- val was approaching the .homet team again attacked the visitors' citadel, when Aspinall was successful in scoring. Half-time; War- rington, 3; Altrincham, After a• short interval the game was resumed and theohome team were soon the aggressors, and Pearson, Baldwin and Hartley 'each cleared in turn and a visit was paid to the home- end, but was not of long duration, and the ball was soon in the vicinity of the ,visi- tors' goal again and AsPiriall Was once more suocessful in steering the ball safely past Pearson. The home team now appeared to have get the visitors well in hand and the game was chiefly in their half, and, Banner centring well from near the flag, Woodhouse added- the goal. From the centre-kick the visitors' centre-forward tried desperately to get through and gained a free-kick just out- side the penalty area. FouldS took the kick and sent in a strong shot, which, Kite tipped over the bar, The resulting corner-kick saw the home defenders clear, and Siddall, raining possession, made a splendid individual effort, beatiag several opponents and nally scoring with a good ghat. Play was still in the viei- tors' half and Pearson and his backs , were hard pressed and several corner-kick' were conceded, but not •improved' upon until even- tually Whitfield found the net with a low .drive from twenty yards' range. The pace now slackened and easy exchanges were seen in 'midfield for a while; then as time was draw- ing near the home team returned to the attack again, and Banner' headed over the bar. Further shots were successfully dealt with by Pearson from Woodhouse and Illidge,, but at length: Siddall scored from a' sharp angle, and a few minute later time was called with the final reult:—Warrington Town, 8 goals; Altrincham, 0.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>THEW' ON AT I,IILDEIPOOL.</head>
        <p>A THEW' ON AT I,IILDEIPOOL.</p>
        <p>Atherton are due to meet the Town at Wilders- pool to-day, and the home team will be selected from :---Kite; Leah, Mill Ward ; Stevens, Whit- field, Rodgers, Payne; Lloyd, Illidge, Siddall. Aspinall, Woodhouse, Banner 'and Tom Whit- field •(home on leave).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>TOWN IDLE ON MONDAY.</head>
        <p>THE TOWN IDLE ON MONDAY.</p>
        <p>Warrington Town should have met Manches- ter United Reserve on Monday, but the match will not take place owing to the fixture clash- ing with that of the Rugby team.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>ST. ELPHIN'S v. GRAPPENHALL.</head>
        <p>ST. ELPHIN'S v. GRAPPENHALL.</p>
        <p>Teams representing the above clubs met last week on the Steel-street Ground in their re- turn engagement, under wintry conditions, and owing to the rain and snow of the morning part the ground wat not in a very good con, dition when the following players lined out:— St. Elphi n's : Povey; Tunstall, Furness; England, Miller; Gle.ave; Watson, Hare, Lee, Boyle and Shelmerdine. Grappenhal I : Thornton; Aikenhead, Rigby; Gibson, Jones, Kenwright; Willoughby,, Haw- thorne, Knight and Watson. • Referee, Mr. IL Berry.</p>
        <p>The "Saints" won the toss and Hawthorne set the ball in motion for the visitors on a treacherous ground. After ' the opening ex- changes the "Saints" settled down to their game and attacked strongly, Lee opening the score after five minutes' play. After this re- verse the visitors, who had only. ten players, broke away on the right, and Willoughby cen- treing nicely, Jones accepted the opening and made the scores equal. After this the game was contested, in strenuous fashion and Lee again put the "Saints" in front. From the centre-kick the visitors made desperate efforts to equalise, but failed to beat the "Saints" defence. Half-time: St. Elphin's, 2; Grappen- hall, 1. On resuming, the, "Saints" soon showed superior form and the combination of Hare and Lee caused trouble to the visitor? defence and resulted in the former increasing the "Saints'" lead. From this point the "Saints" appeared to have much their own way and the visitors' goal was strongly bombarded, and Lee, meeting an accurate centre from Watson, scored the "Saint?" fourth goal. From the centre-kick the visitors led an attack on the home goal, and Willoughby, with only povey to beat, shot wide of the goal. Flair was again quickly taken to the visitors' end and Gleave scored the fifth goal. Further pressure followed on the visitors' goal and resulted in Shelmerdine further increasing the " Saints' " lead. Play continued in the visitors' half until the call of time, when the final result was :2—St. Elphin'e, 6 goals; Grappenhall,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>. LATCHFORD RECS. v. LATCHFORD</head>
        <p>. LATCHFORD RECS. v. LATCHFORD</p>
        <p>CADETS.</p>
        <p>The above teams should have met last Saturday, but owing to the inclement weather the. fixture was postponed until Chrietmas morning, when, in dull weather, Mr. J. Gleave took charge of the following players : Recs..: Lockhart; Brandwood, Massey; 'Trainor, Cooper,' Hubbersty; Warburton, Green, Brears, Willcox and 'James. Cadets: Chorlton; Fellows, Paton; Barrow, Jones, Hinton; Willoughby, Heesom, Daven- port, Littler and Ashurst. The Rees. lost the toss and had to play against a slight wind. Brears set the ball rolling and the opening exchanges were evenly contested. Then the Rees. assumed the aggres- sive and the Cadets' goal was in grave danger from a free-kick just outside the penalty area. However, no Kite resulted and play was trans- ferred to mid-field, A. good run by Heesom was neatly ,checked by. Massey when the for- mer was getting near the goal. Play was re- turned to the Cadets' end and Fellows conceded a corner-kick, which proved fruitless, and directly after Green failed to accept an easy opening and a weak shot by James went wide. By judicious passing Jones enabled his for- wards to attack the. Recs? goal, but Brandwood and Massey were successful in clearing their lines. The Cadets continued to press and forced an unproductive corner and the ball was quickly transferred to the opposite end, where Green was again at fault by shooting o•.-er the bar when well placed. From now the Recs. applied more pressure, and after "several shots had been repulsed, Willcox opened the score for the Rees., and a few minutes later thia was supplemented by Green scoring from a penalty kick. James added a third goal after the goalkeeper' had' made a good save. The Cadets retaliated by attacking keenly and good shots by Hessom and Jones were just wide of the goal. Half-time Recs., 3; Cadets, 0.</p>
        <p>The game was cluickly resumed and the Recs. were soon attacking and after CliOrlton had made a couple of good eaves Wilcox ,steered the hall past him. For a while the game was not very interesting and play was chiefly in mid-field: Towards the close of play the Recs. resumed the offensive and Willcox scored three times in succession. Result : Tees., 6; Cadets, 0.</p>
        <p>HIGHFIELD JUNIORS v. ST. ELPHIN'S.</p>
        <p>This re-arranged fixture between the above clubs was played on Boxing Day, on the ground of the former and before a record "gate." The following players lined out:— H ighfield Juniors: Pimdlebury; Hughes, Bell; Page, Hankey, Wishlade; Pritchard, Moulsdale, Horton, Ford and Tomlinson. St. Elphin's: Povey; Tunstall, Furness; Eng- land, Miller, Gleavi; Boyle, Hare; Lee, Wat- son and Shelznerdine, Referee, Mr. H. Berry.</p>
        <p>The "Saints" won the toss, and after kees exchanges in mid-field the home team became dangerous and a good shot by Ford very nearly took effect. From the ,goal-kick the visitors took up the aggressive and Watson tested Pendlebury with a long shot, which was got away with some difficulty. 'Then the home forwards transferred-play and a good position was lost owing to Mouledale fouling Gleave, and from the free-kick the "Saints" became dangerous, but Pendlebury again saved well and even play in mid-field followed. The home team gradually worked the hall tcwards the visitors' goal and Pritchard sent in a stinging shot, which caused Povey to concede a corner- kick. Wiehlade placed the ball accurately and Horton had not much difficult; in heading the ball safely past Povey, thus gaining the first goal for the home team. From the centre- kick the "Saints" retaliated keenly, and after exciting .exchanges Boyle equalised with a pplendid:Shot,from the tonek-line. Both teams were neve'.off'' their mettkao• gain the lead, and the home team, pqNtng the stronger, made a strong attack on the "Saints" goal and forced another corner, Tomlinson placing the ball with good judgment, a keen 'struggle followed in the goalmonth, and eventually Ford scored the second :goal. Half-tinie:—. Highfield Juniors, 2; S. Elphin's, I. The second portion opened by a strong at- tack on the home goal and a corner-kick was -gained, but the "Saints" failed to improve on it and the home forwards. gaining posses- sion, Tomlinson and Horton combined well together and transferred to the " Saints' " goal, and the latter beat Povey with a fast law shot. This reverse nettled the "Saints," and they played with more determination and increased the pace, but found they were up against, a stout pair of defenders in Hughes and Bell, and despite their efforts they failed to reduce the arrears and at the call of time the final result was :—Highfield Juniors, 3; St. Elphin's, 1.</p>
        <p>WIDNES JUNIORS v. ORFORD JUNIORS. This postponed fixture between the above clubs was played on Boxing Day, on the Tan- house-lane ground. The visitors had only ten players and the clubs were represpitted as follows:</p>
        <p>Wienes Juniors: Roy's; llotiltinson, Case; Gililooley, Hill, Price; Scott, Rollins, Fearns, Cox and "forty.</p>
        <p>:,,Orford Juniors: Walters; Gibson, Witton;</p>
      </div>
