      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0176" n="art0176">
        <head>Money.</head>
        <p>Money.</p>
        <p>Medical.</p>
        <p>THE ESTATE MARKET.</p>
        <p>Some Interesting Comments On The Business 01 1916.</p>
        <p>"The past year has proved that British real estate is the pre-eminent form of investment. After two and a half years of war there. is no great difficulty in dealing with any form of property ,which shows a fair return for capital, whether in the nature of investment or pleasur- able occupation. "Ground rents and the higher class form of investment property are naturally 'affected by the fact that 6 per cent. is offered by the Gov- ernment for money. Comparatively few have been put in. the open ,market, and of those sold, ground rents have realised generally under than over ,20 years' purchase, and Other forms of property investment must show Over 6 per cent. to he attractive. .</p>
        <p>"That the estate market is as buoyant as it is under prevailing circumstances is wonderful, and shows, as hardly anything else could show, the unabated confidence of the people in the stability of the country."—Comments of leading firm of London Estate Agents.</p>
        <p>We un.dersta.nd:the same comment is substan- tiated by local estate agents and valuers, who have had experience, in dealing with local pro- perties during the past year. The demand for good cottage property has been fairly good, whilst investments for pleasurable occupation has been exceptionally, good, thus proving that the above statements of the provincial authori- ties'are reliable, and that real estate is still the leading and pre-40inent•form of investment for the people, in milt° of the prevailing adverse</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0177" n="art0177">
        <head>TREVELYAN.</head>
        <p>TREVELYAN.</p>
        <p>Rev. H. Ogden's Reply To</p>
        <p>Councillor Dale.</p>
        <p>MR. DALE'S REJOINDER.</p>
        <p>To the Editor of "The Examiner."</p>
        <p>Sir,—l rejoice in the spirit of general fair- ness in which. your correspondent, Mr. Dale, has written a letter concerning an address de- livered by myself 'upon the sanctity .of free speech. I venture to believe that had the same - cool; reflective mood been uppermost in his ' mind at an earlier date, he would not have shared in organising the destruction of a meeting which had not only a legal but a moral right to be held. I am most interested in that portion of his letter in which he attempts to prove that I do not believe in free 'speech because I suggested that "outside hideous blasphemy and rank ignorance I hardly knew when free speech was not sacred." I regret that Mr. Dale should have quibbled over a mere technical point when matters of great principle were in- volved. Blasphemy surely can have no moral urge. A man pouring out verbal filth cannot be moved by great' ethical motives. Mr. Dale surely • does not suggest that Mr. Trevelyan had sunk so low' as to have no m4al motive higher than a blasphemer. Mr. Dale does himself .and his cause a distinct disservice by ' the very. argument that he uses, because his advocacy' would seem to imply that if..a peace advocate is to be permitted to. utter all that is within him in these times, an equal' right should be given to a villainous foul' tongue. One is actuated by distinct moral motives, the other by what is immoral and unclean. Does not Mr. Dale see that free speech is sacred because of its moral basis, When the present Prime Minister went to Birmingham during the 'Boer War, Birmingham considered that Mr. Lloyd George was placing the nation in peril and Birmingham smashed his meeting, and the Prime Minister was fortunate in not having been smashed himself in the process. Has the Prime Minister ever disowned his action? Was he moved by no' higher consider- ation than a blasphemer? Was Birmingham right in its attitude? If Birmingham was wrong during the Boer War, Warrington was wrong a week or two ago, The moral motive gives the sanctity to free speech, and though I believe Mr. Trevelyan to be a misguided man I cannot believe other than that he is a' man moved by the highest principles and the most sacred motives.</p>
        <p>When Mr. Dale -says that ." we cannot be over-fastidious in these days," and "we can- not stay to be over-Particular about conven- tionalism," he betrays his woeful misappre- hension of, the great moral sanction of free speech. Truth is life, : and terms such as fastidious and conventional are related to the bargains and customs of every day. But man cannot barter and bargain with truth. I would suggest that my friend, Mr. Dale, sound a deeper note. Why Mr.: Dale should quote Mr. Henderson in this controversy I hardly know. I did not suggest that peace at present was possible. I did not suggest that Mr. Trevelyan's arguments were sound; but I am concerned that a sacred right, should be maintained, even though that sacred right at this moment is not popular.— Yours faithfully,</p>
        <p>H. OGDEN. 31, Orford-road, December 22nd, 1916. [The above letter was received last week, but to late for publication.] t</p>
        <p>"RANK IGNORANCE" AND "HIDEOUS BLASPHEMY."</p>
        <p>To the Editor of "The Examiner."</p>
        <p>Sir,—The Rev. IL Ogden's recent letter on "The Sanctity of Free Speech" confirms my impression that in principle he and I agree. Like myself, he does not believe in free speech in the Absolute sense; his letter clearly proves this. It looks as though, in thefirst instance, Mr. Ogden had rushed at his subject without having -given it sufficient thought,, and that he is now anxious to justify his attitude. His efforts to extricate himself from .an un- fortunate position remind one. of .the experi- ence of Pliable. His gtruggling causes. him .to sink deeper in the slough, and, like /liable, if he can get out, though. it be on the wrong side, he doesn't seem to mind. The diatribe on blasphemy. is Very inappro- priate, as I did not mention the word in my letter. .</p>
        <p>Mr. Ogden argues that free .speech should be sanctioned, with certain exceptions. But the exceptions allowed are so wide and far-reaching as Completely to'destroy his original argument.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0178" n="art0178">
        <p>They are—mark the adjectives—" rank ignor- ance" and "hideous blasphemy." We are not told who is to decide when the one becomes rank and the other hideous. It is evidently a question Of degree, with strong yet vague adjectives as the determining factors. And no elite is given as to how a common standard of comparison may be discovered as a guide when opinions differ. We cannot assume that all sensible people are agreed as to whit constitutes "rank ignor- ance." Learned men, including politicians and theologians, often accuse each other of "rank ignorance." With regard to Mr. Ogdexes comment on my supposed misapplication of the words " fasti- dious " and "conventionalism," I think, if he will consult a good tnodern dictionary, he will discover that my 1190 of the words was not only permissible, but appropriate. It is rather surprising that one who professes to be a strong supporter of free speech should be so "fastidious" about .the use of words Yours truly,</p>
        <p>G. DALE.</p>
        <p>23, Lovely-lane, December 28th.</p>
      </div>
