      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0181" n="art0181">
        <head>LOCAL WEDDINGS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL WEDDINGS.</p>
        <p>SMITH-BENNETI.</p>
        <p>The marriage was solemnised at Bold-street: Wesleyan Church on Saturday of Mr. George Davey Smith, of King-street, Warrington, third son of Mr.. Smith, of Kirton Lindsey, Lin- colnshire; and Miss Emma MaY Bennett, eldest daughter of, Mr. E. Bennett, the Grange, San- key. The .Itev. Percy meson officiated. The bride, wh.o was given away by her father, wore a cream alpaca dress with hat to match, trimmed with skunk. She carried a bouquet composecl .of orange .blossom. The bridesmaids were the Misses Nora and Mabel Bennett (sisters of the bride). Their dresses were of violet cre- tonne, and they carried bouquets. Mr. T. H. Carrutheth tided as best man. Mr. Frank Hick- man was at the organ and played festal music. The reception was held at the Grange, Sankey, after which Mr. and Mrs. Smith left for Derby- shire. The bride travelled in 'a navy blue cos- tume with veleur ,hat to match. The bride- groom's gift to the bride was a set of furs and cheque.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0182" n="art0182">
        <head>TANNER-DALE.</head>
        <p>TANNER-DALE.</p>
        <p>A wedding was solemnised at the Working Men's Mission, Bank Quay, on, Tuesday after- noon, betiveen Mr. Tom Tanner, oldest son of Mr. and Mrs. B. C. Tanner, of Winwiek-street, and Miss Olive Mary Dale, youngest daughter of Mr. and Mrs. Thomas. Dale, 'of Priestley- street.</p>
        <p>The service was conducted by the Rev. P. meson. Mr. Fred Atkinson presided at the organ. The 'bride was given away by her father, and was daintily attired in.. white 'satin and ninon, with silver trimming. She wore a veil, surmounted with a wreath of orange blossom, and carried a sheaf Of lilies. The bridesmaids (Miss Beatrice Dale and Miss Eva Tanner) were pleasingly attired in pale pink and pale blue silk crepe respectiVely, and carried bouquets, of narcissus and pink roses. • • •</p>
        <p>The best man: was Signaller Ben Farrar, of the Queen's Westminster The wedding breakfast ,was served in the schoolroom adjoining the Chapel, about,6o guests being present Thelappy.couple left later in the evening for short honeymoon in Blackpool, prior to the bridegroom .reporting for service with the Colours,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0183" n="art0183">
        <head>RICKERS-PARDOE.</head>
        <p>RICKERS-PARDOE.</p>
        <p>A pretty wedding took -place at the Latchford Wesleyan Church, on Tuesday, when Miss Lilian kardoe, daughter Of Mr., and Mrs. B. Pardoe:lof 4, Powell-street, was married to Mr. C. S. Rickers:second son of Mr. and Mrs. E. Rickers, of .184, WeMeld-street. The Rev. A. J. Morton was the officiating min- ister,' and Miss Hartley was at the organ. The bride Was attired in an ivory silk crepe-de-chine dress, trinithed with silk braid. She also Wore a 'Brussels net lace ,veil, crowned:with a wreath of orange blossom, and carried a sheaf of lilies. Miss M. Boyer (cousin of the bride), Miss M. Pardo() (sister of the bride), and Miss A. Rickers (sister of the bridegroom) acted as bridesmaids. and wore white dresses of embroidered voile and white felt hats. Eitel' carried a bouquet of pink chrysanthemums Mr. E. G. Rickers, brother of the bridegroom, performed the. duties of best man- The happy couple are spending. their honeymoon at Ilfracombe.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0184" n="art0184">
        <head>Plenty to do.</head>
        <p>Plenty to do.</p>
        <p>Ililitary Officer (giving instructions to his: orderly, a new recruit): "You rise at five, o'clock, shave yourself, and clean your boots and equipment. Then you clean my boots, but- tons,)6lt, &amp;c., shaTe me, A'ee to my horse, which you inust groom thoroughly, and clean the equipment. After that you go to your hut, help to serve the breakfast, and after breakfast lend a hand washing-up. At eight o'clock you go on parade and drill till twelve—" Recruit whose face had been growing longer) "Beg pardon, sir, but is there anyone else in the army besides me?,". ! •</p>
