      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0185" n="art0185">
        <head>somiIimmoNIIMMIIIMIIIIMIMINIMIIMMIIMMNIIIIMIIMMIIIIM.IMINNaIfti</head>
        <p>somiIimmoNIIMMIIIMIIIIMIMINIMIIMMIIMMNIIIIMIIMMIIIIM.IMINNaIfti</p>
        <p>STAGELAND.</p>
        <p>Royal Court.</p>
        <p>"The Toreador," having been a sleeping beauty for 'many, many years, has awakened, and sparkles in rare splendour at . the Royal Court Theatre this week. Few seats have been exempt from occupation, and Mr. Harry War- burton, the manager, has had many words whis- pered into his ear in appreciation of the de- lightful production he booked.</p>
        <p>As many as forty ladies take part, and all are resplendent in dresses as new and up-to-date •as the scenery. The lead is not confined to just one or two artistes, seven or eight of them each "having a basin,:" as theatricals term parts of fairly large dimen- sions. The girls in the chorus, one presumes, have tea cups.</p>
        <p>Upon the company they have gathered Messrs. Macdonald and Young may be congratulated. In Mr. Robert Needham as Carajolo—the Toreador—they have got not only One who plays a romantic part, but one whose career has been a romance, too. A New Zealander by birth, his experience has beeii very varied. He ap- peared in public as a singer as early as five years old, and off and on has since that time dis- played his splendid vocal power and stage pres- ence. He has played one week in a comic opera and bean boundary riding in the bush the next.</p>
        <p>It is twelve or fifteen years since Mr. Need- ham played the title role in "The Toreador." In the meantime he has played in other musical comedies, and daring his eight years' residence in England has taken leading parts in the "Merry Widow," "Gipsy Love," "The Mar- riage Market," and others. It is interesting to uote that Mr. Frank Forster, the tenor, who is charming audiences at the Hippodrome this week, is his cousin. •</p>
        <p>The ladies are also of high-class quality. These include three sweet and pretty lilies—Miss Lily Alaine as Donna Teresa, Miss Lily Ward as Susan (she is remembered with pleasure as a principal in "Miss Hook of Holland "), Miss Lily Leverne as Mrs. Mahon Hoppings, and Miss Florence Helm as Nancy Staunton.</p>
        <p>Miss Helm is equally at home in a gentle- man's suit as in her pretty West Bud frocks. She is a lady who will get on in the aleatrical world. Born of theatrical parents, she was in " Peggy " at the Gaiety, London, when only fifteen years of age, and later at the London Empire. She has been with Robert Court- neidge's company in "The Arcadians" and the " Mousme."</p>
        <p>Not yet twenty-one years of age, she has also toured with Mr. Horace Hodges in "Grumpy," Old has beei in revues, being the leading lady in "Lancashire and Proud of It "—a revue pre- sented at the Warrington Hippodrome twelve months ago. ller refinement and charm are well remembered.</p>
        <p>Last summer she was also a trick rider on a high jumping steed, and is the first girl to play the part of Dick Turpin on the stage—and the sketch has been running for over one hundred years. I may mention that Miss Helm used to call herself " Babg." This week she has realised that she has grown, and has empowered the en- terprising business manager, Mr. Alfred Drink. water, to bill her as Miss Florence Helm.</p>
        <p>In securing the pantomime "Dick Whitting- ton" for the Royal Court Theatre next week, Mr. Warburton has been extremely fortunate, for those who have seen it at Birkenhead Hip- podrome this week assure me that it is of high- class quality and full of revelty. With Miss Edna Latonne as principal boy, it could not fail to be a Success, for she is an artiste of note— a7' star " artiste, in fact. "A good, clean pan- tomime" is the._claim made for "Dick Whit- tington," by Mr. Walter Bishop, the producer.</p>
        <p>Mr, Bishop himself is in the cast, and sup- plies a great deal of humour, in which depart- ment lie is well supported by Wheeler and Wil- son as Captain and Mate. respectively.</p>
        <p>The following week " Betty " pays a return visit. "Betty after Dick Whittington," ae a wag said.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0186" n="art0186">
        <head>Palace and Hippodrome.</head>
        <p>Palace and Hippodrome.</p>
        <p>A big vaudeville menu is presented nightly at the Hippodrome and, the management is to be congratulated on securing such a big list of first-class attractions for Christmas week. Qiuto a pantomime colour is given to the show by the lively music rendered by the orchestra, and the selection of popular melodies arranged by the Musical Director, Mr. T. Ridley, forms quite, a pleasing interlude during the perform- ance.</p>
        <p>Not for a long while have we had such a fine display of gymnastic strength as that given by Wilson and Ernie, who' hail from Scandinavia, and certainly possess a vast amount of that muscle and toughness which one naturally associates with the brawny North. They are assisted in the performance by volunteers from the audience, and the sometimes unavailing efforts of these enthusiasts to raise heavy- weights and manipulate gigantic bar-bells is provocative of much .amusement. On one par- ticular evening a Jack Tar somewhat aston- ished,the house, and, indeed, the experts them- selves, with the plucky and partially successful way in which he tackled, some of the trying feats.</p>
        <p>Quite a rousing turn is that given I&gt;y 'Kelly, West and Reed, in which figure a piano, a fool, a soubrette and an acrobat. The piano, how- ever, is played on with the feet, being, in fact, a concealed spring mattress device for acrobatic Work of a clever character. A lot of clever "business" is introduced into an exceptionally good performance.</p>
        <p>Will: Hay is another favourite as the school- master comedian, an.l in the comical- absurdity "The Tutor and the Scholar" keeps the audi- ence in roars of laughter. Equally successful in the art of fun-making are Larkum Bros., who appear in a knockabout sketch, "The New Porter "—a very, amusing conception. which "catches on ". tremendously.</p>
        <p>Noland and Kiddie vary the programme with a refined and, attractive dancing performance. The little girl is really remarkable for the ease and neatness with which she. goes through her steps. There is a vast amount of •promise in this ohild dancer, judging from her agility and se 1f- p os Bess io n. Alma le Dare has some "catchy" choruses in her' latest successes, and West and Tate, introduce some - athletic novelties.</p>
        <p>A special, paragraph must be reserved for Frank Fostbr,. a fine and strikingly sweet tenor, who comes to us with laurels won at Covent Garden and leading .concert halls. His voice is of excellent range and tone, and, while it is heard to great advantage in ballads of a deli- cate nature, it is more successful in, declama- tory music. This fact is especially noticeable in his splendid rendering of that famous song "The Death of Nelson "—Sims iteeves' favour- ite contribution—amongst a number of other imperishable ballads. Mr. Foster has had a most flattering reception during thgeweek and certainly deserves it.</p>
        <p>Heading an attractive bill at the Hippodrome next week is Leslie Lynn and Co. in their great comedy sketch "Rogers," which is bound to score a great "hit." J. J. Wild's company in the new Lancashire comedy, "'Listing for a Soldier," is something out of the ordinary, as also is Frederick Culpitt, the migical comedian. The rest of the programme announced 'includes: RiChe and Walker, the new ekyle American dancing boys;. Le More and his thought-reading dog; and Duig Seu Bros., Chinese acrobats, FOYER.</p>
      </div>
