        <p>ROMANCE OF SAVINGS BANKS AVINGS BANKS, at their inception over a hundred years ago, partook of the nature of voluntary institutions, and are very largely so I I , to-day. They were founded by benevolent people taking a leading part in the affairs of their respective neighbourhoods, and actuated by a desire to help their neighbours to save money. One of the earliest names mentioned in connection with . the first proposals for the establishment of such banks is that of a clergyman, and, curiously enough, Sunday evening was the time chosen for receiving deposits. This, we are told, was doubtless in view of their motto : " Upon the first day of the week, let every one of you lay by him in store, as God hath prospered him." That motto was taken seriously to heart, with the result that Savings Banks have prospered exceedingly. Our own local Savings Bank has just issued its Ninety-ninth Annual Report, wherein it is stated that there are now 19,840 depositors. You will see by the proceedings of the Annual Meeting—reported elsewhere—how successful the institution has become and why you should join at once. At YOUR SERVICE. Warrington, Runcorn and District Savings Bank. BUTTERMARKET STREET. RUNCORN OFFICE: 41, HIGH STREET. BRANCHES AT BANK QUAY AND EARLESTOWN.</p>
